{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_016doaP6zhfjFAFf9aUFHjmg","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Paper Framing — nlp_variance_gtheory\n2\t\n3\t## Target Venue\n4\t- Primary: AAAI 2027 (abstract Jul 21, paper Jul 28 — 9 weeks from today)\n5\t- Backup: ACL 2027 via ARR (October 15 cycle — 21 weeks)\n6\t- Extended: TACL (rolling)\n7\t\n8\t## Title\n9\tReproducibility Budgets for LLM Evaluation: A Generalizability Theory Analysis of Variance Sources Across Benchmarks, Models, and Evaluation Metrics\n10\t\n11\t## Core Claims (3 条，可证伪)\n12\t\n13\t**Claim 1 — Model-Conditional Item Dominance**: Item selection is the single largest variance source in single-model LLM evaluation (61.1% on MMLU), but model×item interaction (26.2%) emerges as the second-largest source when model is treated as a crossed facet. Item difficulty rankings are model-specific: two models with similar aggregate accuracy (Llama 66.4% vs Gemma 73.2%) disagree dramatically at the item level — item_id drops from 61% (single-model) to 38% (cross-model) as model×item absorbs the model-conditional portion. This means single-model variance decompositions overestimate item dominance by conflating universal and model-specific item difficulty. Cross-benchmark gradient (pending exp-002) is hypothesized to further modulate this structure.\n14\t**Empirical anchors (exp-001, 4 models, balanced bf16, 345,600 records)**: Single-model item_id=61.1%, cross-model item_id=42.1% [36.0-48.8%] (↓19pp), model×item=33.6% [28.4-37.9%] (second-largest source, increased from 3-model 31.7%). G_item=0.820 [0.782-0.861]. D-study prediction validated out-of-sample: 3-model predicted G=0.816 for n=4, actual=0.820 (Δ=0.004). Forward projection: 5 models → G=0.848, 8→0.893, 10→0.910 (G≥0.90 requires ~10 models). Residual dropped 24%→19.5% as 4th model split residual into model×item. Per-model interaction: Mistral 29.25% (lowest accuracy 63.8%) > Qwen 27.11% > Gemma 23.95% > Llama 19.69%.\n15\t**REML robustness**: REML-EM (500 iter, practical convergence Δ<0.003pp) confirms Henderson I: all components differ <0.5pp. Henderson I unbiased for balanced designs. Dataset B differences (item_id 38→52%, model×item 26→12%) due to design differences (factor selection), not estimator bias — measurement design choices themselves affect observed variance structure.\n16\t**Completed robustness**: BF16-vs-FP16 sensitivity confirms precision→0.0006% without FP32 (FP32 is sole source of precision variance); leave-one-model-out (matched-spec) shows G_item range 0.747-0.793 across 3-model subsets (spread=0.046) — all below 0.80, confirming 4 models is the minimum for reliable cross-model measurement. LOMO also confirms item_id dominance and model×item as second-largest source are stable across all subsets.\n17\t**GLMM cross-check (dual-analysis, must report both)**: Two complementary analyses:\n18\t(i) *Marginal logit ANOVA*: All 7 grouped component rankings preserved exactly (ρ=1.0), consistent with MQL-1 prediction that logit-scaling is approximately constant at p̄=0.70.\n19\t(ii) *VB GLMM (25-item subsample)*: Ranking reversal on top-2 — model:item_id=47.3% > item_id=22.8% on logit scale (ρ=0.571, p=0.18). Attributable to model underspecification (missing 4 item-interaction terms in VB model) + subsample.\n20\t**Paper must present BOTH honestly** — \"marginal logit ANOVA preserves all rankings; VB GLMM shows differences attributable to model underspecification.\" Do NOT overwrite ρ=0.571 with ρ=1.0. Implication: model×item interaction may be even more important than Henderson I indicates on logit scale — strengthens Claim 1's core message.\n21\t\n22\t**Claim 2 — Conditional Separability**: Infrastructure factors (numerical precision) and methodology factors (prompt, seed, temperature, ordering) are approximately separable at the aggregate level — direct infrastructure×methodology interaction ≈0% under binary scoring; the only detectable cross-factor interaction (precision×temperature=1.3%) emerges under text-exact-match scoring, consistent with floating-point non-associativity (Messina & Scotta, 2026). Both factor types interact substantially with item difficulty (precision×item, prompt×item), making their effects item-conditional. This provides a metric-qualified confirmatory finding for isolated-factor studies while warning that conclusions are conditional on item selection and evaluation metric. Note: with only numerical precision tested on a single GPU architecture (pending exp-003 for hardware crossing), \"infrastructure\" is currently limited to the precision facet.\n23\t\n24\t**Claim 3 — Metric-Conditional Reproducibility Budget**: Reproducibility budgets are fundamentally metric-dependent and temperature-conditional. Binary correct: G=0.611 (200 items), G≥0.80 requires ~510 items (all facets random) or ~414 items (practical: precision+temperature fixed). Text-exact-match: G=0.007 (temperature dominates at 79.7%), unreliable at any temperature >0. Temperature-stratified analysis reveals binary G degrades monotonically (T=0.0: 0.760 → T=0.7: 0.549). G-theory requires 3.6-4.4× more items than IRT (510 vs 116) because it accounts for prompt×item (4.6%), seed×item (4.7%), and residual (21.6%) dimensions that IRT ignores. Current practices (1 prompt, 1 seed, ≤200 items) yield G=0.611 — below the 0.80 threshold for dependable measurement.\n25\t\n26\t## Contributions (4 条，明确区分)\n27\t\n28\t1. **Model×item interaction as the hidden dominant source**: In a balanced 4-model design (345,600 records), model×item interaction (33.6%, CI [28.4-37.9%]) is the second-largest variance source, while item_id drops from 61% (single-model) to 42%. Item difficulty rankings are model-specific — single-model G-studies overestimate item dominance by conflating universal and model-specific difficulty. G_item=0.820 [0.782-0.861] with 4 models; D-study prediction validated out-of-sample (3-model predicted 0.816, actual 0.820, Δ=0.004). Reaching G≥0.90 requires ~10 models. [Note: position as quantification of a phenomenon expected from measurement theory, not as a surprising discovery. The novelty is the magnitude, the out-of-sample D-study validation, and the cross-model generalizability implications.]\n29\t\n30\t2. **Precision separability with metric qualification**: Precision×methodology interactions are negligible under binary scoring (<0.1%), providing empirical support for isolated-factor studies. Under text-exact-match scoring, precision×temperature reaches 4.15%, consistent with floating-point non-associativity. Separability is confirmed as metric-conditional. [Note: scope to \"numerical precision on single GPU architecture\" — not \"infrastructure\" broadly. This is a confirmatory secondary finding, not a primary contribution. Pending BF16-vs-FP16 sensitivity to isolate FP32/vLLM confound.]\n31\t\n32\t3. **IRT vs G-theory: the cost of multi-facet reliability**: The first empirical quantification of the multi-facet reliability cost for LLM evaluation: G-theory requires 3.6-4.4× more items than IRT (510 vs 116 for MMLU) because it accounts for prompt, seed, and ordering dimensions that IRT ignores. At IRT's recommended 116 items, G-theory G is only 0.529. This quantifies the \"hidden cost\" of evaluation dimensions invisible to IRT. [Note: keep as numbered contribution — Dream + Reviewer consensus this is one of the paper's strongest findings. Wording adjusted from \"first direct comparison\" to \"first empirical quantification\" per SF6 resolution.]\n33\t\n34\t4. **Practitioner toolkit**: Open-source G-theory variance decomposition pipeline + D-study budget calculator for LLM evaluation. Includes: (a) benchmark-specific reproducibility budget tables (items × prompts × seeds for target G), (b) temperature-stratified reliability guide, (c) model-as-facet analysis template. Addresses \"purely diagnostic\" criticism — provides actionable tools, not just warnings.\n35\t\n36\t## Methodological Robustness Package (6 验证分析)\n37\tAll completed on Llama 239,200 records:\n38\t- **Binary data not artifact**: Null simulation item_id=0.08% vs observed 61.1% (737.5×); obs/Bernoulli ratio=1.0008\n39\t- **Fixed vs random insensitive**: Binary G ranges 0.611-0.664 across all facet classification choices\n40\t- **Bootstrap = jackknife**: CI width ratio 0.96-1.04, independence violation negligible\n41\t- **Temperature stratification**: Binary G degrades T=0.0 (0.760) → T=0.7 (0.549); text metric unreliable at all T>0\n42\t- **D-study formula verified**: Monotonicity assertion added; min_items_G80=414 (practical) / 510 (all random)\n43\t- **IRT comparison**: G-theory 3.6-4.4× more conservative than IRT; difference attributable to prompt/seed/ordering dimensions\n44\t- **REML vs Henderson I**: REML-EM (500 iter) confirms all components within <0.5pp of Henderson I; Henderson I unbiased for balanced designs\n45\t- **BF16-vs-FP16 sensitivity**: Excluding FP32, precision effect drops from 0.4% to 0.0006% (precision×item: 1.3%→0.04%). FP32 anomaly is entirely a vLLM enforce_eager artifact, not a fundamental precision effect. BF16≈FP16 (Δ=0.23pp accuracy)\n46\t- **Leave-one-model-out stability**: All 3-model subsets preserve item_id dominance (39-45%) and model×item as second-largest (32-37%). G_item range 0.747-0.793 (spread=0.046), all below 0.80 — confirming 4 models is the minimum configuration for G≥0.80. This strengthens the D-study \"need ~10 models for G≥0.90\" prediction.\n47\t\n48\t## Story Spine\n49\t\n50\t**Problem**: When LLM evaluation results vary across reproductions, practitioners cannot determine whether the culprit is prompt phrasing, random seed, GPU hardware, numerical precision, temperature, or item selection — let alone how these factors interact. Every prior study isolates at most two sources, implicitly assuming separability.\n51\t\n52\t**Existing approaches & gaps**: Bouthillier et al. (2021) decomposed ML benchmark variance across hyperparameters, data splits, and seeds via ANOVA, establishing the template for factorial variance decomposition in ML evaluation. Mesting (2026) adapted G-theory to 5 methodological facets in LLM evaluation but treated infrastructure as fixed. Yuan et al. (2025) documented up to 9% accuracy variation from hardware/precision without factorial attribution. Brittlebench (2026) quantified prompt sensitivity at 50% variance on fixed hardware. IRT-based approaches (Lalor et al. 2019; Rodriguez et al. 2021; Polo et al. 2024) have analyzed item-level properties and efficient subsampling, but from a different methodological perspective than G-theory. No study has: (a) crossed infrastructure and methodology in a unified G-theory framework, (b) compared variance structure across multiple benchmarks, or (c) provided metric-conditional reproducibility budgets.\n53\t\n54\t**Our framing**: We conduct a multi-benchmark G-theory study crossing infrastructure (numerical precision) × methodology (prompt, seed, temperature, ordering) × measurement (item) factors, with model treated as a crossed facet. The infrastructure crossing yields a confirmatory null result: aggregate separability holds, validating the implicit assumption of prior isolated-factor studies. The primary novel finding is the cross-benchmark variance gradient — item dominance systematically weakens as tasks shift from knowledge retrieval to multi-step reasoning — explaining divergent conclusions in the literature. D-study projections provide the first benchmark-specific, metric-conditional reproducibility budgets for LLM evaluation, directly comparable to IRT-based subsampling approaches.\n55\t\n56\t**Headline result template**: \"In a G-study of 345,600 evaluation records across 4 models on MMLU (200 items × 6 prompts × 6 seeds × 3 temperatures × 4 orderings), item selection accounts for 42% of variance when model is treated as a crossed facet — but model×item interaction (34%) emerges as the second-largest source, revealing that item difficulty rankings are substantially model-specific (G_item=0.82, requiring ~10 models for G≥0.90). A D-study from 3 models predicted the 4-model G coefficient within 0.004 — out-of-sample validation of the D-study methodology. G-theory requires 3.6-4.4× more items than IRT for reliable measurement because it accounts for prompt, seed, and ordering dimensions that IRT ignores. Numerical precision×methodology interactions are negligible under binary scoring (<0.1%). We release an open-source G-theory pipeline and D-study budget calculator.\"\n57\t\n58\t## Hook (Counter-Intuitive Quantified Claim)\n59\t\"Researchers debating whether to standardize prompts, seeds, or GPU hardware for LLM evaluation implicitly assume that their findings will generalize across benchmarks — our G-theory analysis across N benchmarks shows the variance structure itself is benchmark-conditional, rendering any 'universal reproducibility recipe' illusory.\"\n60\t\n61\t## Narrative Strategy\n62\t- **Type**: Cross-domain transfer (psychometrics G-theory → ML evaluation) + Empirical contribution (benchmark-conditional gradient)\n63\t- **Tension**: \"No universal recipe\" — the field seeks standardization but variance structure varies qualitatively across task types\n64\t- **Intro structure**: 4 paragraphs + 3 contributions (field mode from distilled writing patterns)\n65\t- **Signature visual**: Stacked-bar variance decomposition chart across benchmarks (field idiom from Mesting/Brittlebench)\n66\t- **Figure plan** (5 figures):\n67\t  1. **Stacked-bar: single-model vs cross-model variance decomposition** — item_id shrinks from 61% to 42%, model×item appears at 34%, residual drops 24%→20%. The \"reveal\" figure.\n68\t  2. **G-theory crossed design diagram** — facets, levels, crossing structure\n69\t  3. **D-study projection curves** — G vs item count for binary (practical vs all-random) and text metric. Shows G≥0.80 threshold at ~414/510 items.\n70\t  4. **IRT vs G-theory item requirement comparison** — bar chart showing 116 (IRT) vs 510 (G-theory) items, with breakdown of where the extra items go (prompt×item, seed×item, residual)\n71\t  5. **Temperature-stratified G coefficient heatmap** — binary vs text metric × 3 temperatures, showing metric×temperature interaction\n72\t- **Key framing shifts from Dream feedback**:\n73\t  - Infrastructure crossing framed as **confirmatory** (validates isolated-factor studies), not as primary novelty\n74\t  - Item dominance acknowledged as **psychometrically expected**; contribution is quantification + benchmark gradient\n75\t  - Cross-benchmark gradient is the **primary novel finding**\n76\t  - \"Fully crossed\" language qualified to MMLU exp-001 only; other benchmarks use reduced designs\n77\t\n78\t## Venue Fit Risks\n79\t- AAAI: Strong fit for \"evaluation methodology\" track. Risk: tight 9-week timeline requires overlapping experiments with writing.\n80\t- Reviewer concern 1: \"Only open-weight models, not API models\" — We intentionally study open-weight models where infrastructure is controllable; API models (studied by Mesting) have opaque infrastructure. Frame as complementary.\n81\t- Reviewer concern 2: \"G-theory is borrowed methodology, not novel\" — Frame as cross-domain transfer. The novelty is the cross-benchmark variance gradient finding, not G-theory itself.\n82\t- Reviewer concern 3: \"Item dominance is obvious from measurement theory\" — Acknowledge explicitly. Contribution is quantification (X-Y%, pending exp-001/002), benchmark-dependence, and D-study implications. Contextualize against IRT item analysis literature.\n83\t\n84\t## Anticipated Reviewer Concerns & Planned Responses\n85\t1. **\"Only N GPU types\"** → exp-003 (multi-hardware crossing) addresses this. If blocked, explicitly scope to \"numerical precision\" and frame hardware as future work.\n86\t2. **\"float32 engine confound\"** → exp-001 fixes with V0 enforce_eager for all precisions. Confound discovery itself is a standalone methodological contribution.\n87\t3. **\"Only MMLU\"** → exp-002 expands to GSM8K, ARC-Challenge, HellaSwag. Cross-benchmark gradient is the core finding.\n88\t4. **\"Small item sample\"** → exp-001 expands from 50 to 200 items. exp-001b tests resampling stability.\n89\t5. **\"No training-time analysis\"** → exp-006 (fine-tuning G-study with RoBERTa). Explicitly qualify as encoder-only classification; decoder fine-tuning is future work.\n90\t6. **\"Missing scale validation\"** → exp-005 (14B models)\n91\t7. **\"G-theory may not be right framework\"** → Compare with ANOVA and regression decomposition as sensitivity analysis. Cite Bouthillier et al. (2021) as ANOVA-based predecessor; show G-theory adds D-study projections.\n92\t8. **\"Separability claim is metric-dependent\"** → This is precisely the point: reproducibility budgets must be metric-qualified. Replace arbitrary threshold with bootstrap CI on interaction term.\n93\t9. **\"FP32 degradation is already known (Yuan 2025)\"** → Position as supporting finding with novel item-level breakdown. Not an independent claim.\n94\t10. **\"Incremental over Mesting (2026)\"** → Infrastructure crossing is confirmatory (valuable as validation, not breakthrough). Primary novelty: cross-benchmark gradient, task-specific budgets, dual-metric analysis, confound fix. Mesting = single benchmark, no infrastructure; we = multi-benchmark, infrastructure confirmation + benchmark gradient.\n95\t11. **\"Model not treated as G-theory facet\"** → exp-004 includes model-as-facet reanalysis of MMLU data. Report model×item interaction separately from residual.\n96\t12. **\"No CIs on variance components\"** → exp-004 includes bootstrap CIs (1000 resamples) on all variance components in all tables.\n97\t13. **\"Missing related work — IRT, tinyBenchmarks, Bouthillier\"** → Comprehensive bibliography (25+ refs). Position D-study budgets against IRT-based subsampling. Cite Bouthillier et al. as ANOVA predecessor.\n98\t14. **\"Diagnostic tool claim unsubstantiated\"** → Release analysis pipeline code (G-theory variance decomposition + D-study projections) as reusable tool. Or reframe as \"empirical analysis + data release.\"\n99\t15. **\"Fine-tuning uses encoder-only RoBERTa\"** → Explicitly qualify. Decoder fine-tuning is a natural extension requiring substantially more compute.\n100\t\n101\t## Design Decisions\n102\t- **Judge model fixed**: Unlike Mesting who includes judge as a facet, we fix the judge to isolate infrastructure × methodology interactions. Judge variance (Mesting: 43.8%) would dominate and obscure the effects we study. Adding judge is a natural extension for future work.\n103\t- **Generation-based evaluation**: Following Hua et al. (EMNLP 2025), we avoid log-likelihood scoring to eliminate scoring artifacts that inflate apparent prompt sensitivity.\n104\t- **Default vLLM settings**: We use default vLLM settings (without batch-invariant kernels) to reflect realistic practitioner conditions, making CUDA-level nondeterminism a measurable variance source.\n105\t- **vLLM 0.9.2**: Required for Blackwell GPU (sm_100/sm_120) + CUDA 12.8 support.\n106\t- **Model as crossed quasi-random facet**: In exp-001 analysis, model is treated as a crossed quasi-random facet — purposive sample from 4 major architecture families (Llama, Gemma, Mistral, Qwen), not a random draw from all LLMs. Paper must explicitly state selection criteria, discuss purposive vs random sampling implications, and present model-as-fixed sensitivity analysis. D-study Δ=0.004 out-of-sample prediction supports exchangeability assumption. Plan to add 2-3 models (Yi-1.5-9B, OLMo-2-7B, etc.) to strengthen to 6-7 models per MF2 resolution.\n107\t- **Reduced designs for non-MMLU benchmarks**: exp-002 uses 2 precisions × 2 temperatures (vs 3×3 in exp-001) due to GPU budget constraints. Explicitly described as \"reduced crossing\" rather than \"fully crossed.\"\n108\t- **Gemma2 float16 exclusion**: Gemma2 architecture does not support float16 inference (vLLM numerical instability guard). Cross-model analysis uses bf16+fp32 (balanced). fp16 data from Llama/Mistral/Qwen used in within-model analyses. bf16≈fp16 difference <0.1pp (D005).\n109\t\n110\t## Methodological Defenses (Dream #2 审稿问题逐条应对)\n111\t\n112\t### MD1. Binary Data in G-theory\n113\t**Issue**: Standard G-theory assumes continuous, approximately normal response variables. Binary 0/1 data violates this; item variance is mechanically linked to item difficulty via Bernoulli p(1-p).\n114\t**Defense strategy (upgraded per Round 1 MF1 + Reviewer + GLMM results)**: Four-line defense, reordered by strength:\n115\t(a) **PRIMARY — Simulation-based recovery** ✅ DONE (matched design verified): Probit latent model → binary, 100 replications × {50,100,200} items × {0.5-0.8} accuracy. **Strongest finding (matched 1,296-condition design): binary ρ=0.732 ≈ continuous ρ=0.741 at n=200 (Δ=0.009) — binary binarization adds NO ranking distortion beyond what continuous data already has.** Henderson I identifies item_id as #1 systematic source in 99-100% of runs. Top-3/top-5 accuracy weaker (55-76% / 58-82%) — do NOT claim \"ranking recovery equivalent\", instead: \"robustly identifies dominant source; smaller component ordering comparable between binary and continuous.\" **Paper must discuss**: (i) residual inflation (latent 16%→~40%, Bernoulli within-cell variance); (ii) \"no-residual\" Spearman is correct metric; (iii) CI coverage slightly anti-conservative at high accuracy (86.4% at p=0.8).\n116\t(b) **COMPLEMENTARY — GLMM logistic-link**: GLMM (Jiang & Skorupski 2017) on 25-item MMLU subsample showed **ranking reversal**: model:item_id #1 (47.3%) > item_id #2 (22.8%) on logit scale, vs Henderson I item_id #1 (42.1%) > model:item_id #2 (33.6%). Spearman ρ=0.571, p=0.18 — rankings NOT statistically consistent. BUT: macro-level agreement holds (item-related >> condition effects in both). Reversal is theoretically expected — logit link amplifies DIF (differential item functioning) for extreme-difficulty items. **Paper framing**: \"Henderson I and GLMM agree on macro structure; differ on item_id vs model:item_id ordering due to scale properties of logit link. This is a complementary perspective, not a contradiction. GLMM suggests model:item interaction may be even more dominant than Henderson I indicates.\" Do NOT claim GLMM \"validates\" Henderson I.\n117\t(c) **Bernoulli-adjusted decomposition**: ✅ DONE. Percentage decomposition inherently normalized by p(1-p) ceiling. Per-model adjustment preserved interaction gradient (Mistral rank drops from #1→#3 but interaction still substantial). Cross-accuracy resampling (0.62-0.72) shows item_id% stable (r=-0.048 with accuracy).\n118\t(d) **Logprob robustness check**: Continuous metric analysis showing gradient preserved — bypasses binary assumption entirely.\n119\t(e) **Methods subsection**: \"Binary Data Assumptions\" discussing Henderson I bias properties for balanced designs, citing Brennan Ch.12, Shavelson et al. 1989, Mushquash & O'Connor 2006, Lalor et al. 2019 (IRT on binary NLP data). Must note GLMM residual drops to 0.004% as Bernoulli variance absorbed by link function.\n120\t\n121\t### MD2. Fixed vs Random Facet Sensitivity\n122\t**Issue**: Temperature (2-3 design points) and ordering (4 systematic rotations) are not randomly sampled from a universe — treating them as random facets may be inappropriate.\n123\t**Defense strategy**: Run sensitivity analysis with temperature and ordering as fixed facets. If variance decomposition changes <5%, report as robust. If substantial, discuss implications and present both analyses. This directly addresses Reviewer concern.\n124\t\n125\t### MD3. Separability Claim Scope\n126\t**Issue**: Separability (<0.3%) holds for knowledge benchmarks but not reasoning tasks (GSM8K=3.0%, MATH=2.5%).\n127\t**Defense**: Qualify all separability claims as \"under binary scoring on knowledge-retrieval benchmarks.\" Present benchmark-conditional separability as a finding (not a limitation): \"separability itself varies across task types.\"\n128\t\n129\t### MD4. D-study Optimization Documentation\n130\t**Issue**: Optimization procedure undocumented; Table 10 arithmetic anomaly (fewer items → higher G).\n131\t**Defense**: Document grid search procedure (bounds, step sizes, objective function). Verify and explain any non-monotonicity (likely due to joint optimization across facets where reallocating item budget to prompt/seed is more efficient).\n132\t\n133\t### MD5. G-coefficient Interpretation\n134\t**Issue**: G=0.22 for 200 items ≠ \"MMLU is unreliable\" (standard MMLU uses ~14K items).\n135\t**Defense**: (a) Clearly distinguish \"G for our 200-item subsample design\" from \"reliability of full MMLU\"; (b) Report D-study projection to 14K items; (c) Frame G=0.22 as \"cost of subsampling\" rather than \"MMLU is broken.\"\n136\t\n137\t### MD6. Bootstrap Independence\n138\t**Issue**: Item-level bootstrap with replacement creates ~127 unique items per 200-item resample, violating independence.\n139\t**Defense**: (a) Implement delete-1 jackknife as comparison; (b) If bootstrap and jackknife CIs are similar (within 20%), report as robust; (c) Discuss limitation in Methods.\n140\t\n141\t### MD7. IRT vs G-theory Comparison\n142\t**Issue**: tinyBenchmarks recommends ~100 items, D-study recommends 420. 4× discrepancy unexplained.\n143\t**Defense**: Direct comparison showing G-theory accounts for prompt/seed/ordering dimensions that IRT ignores. The 4× difference is the \"cost of multi-facet reliability\" — G-theory's budget is larger because it targets reliability across ALL facets, not just items. This comparison is a potential standalone contribution.\n144\t\n145\t### MD8. Cross-benchmark Design Asymmetry\n146\t**Issue**: MMLU uses 3 prec × 3 temp, other benchmarks use 2 × 2.\n147\t**Defense**: Re-analyze MMLU with matched 2×2 design (bf16/fp16 × T=0.0/T=0.7) and compare variance decomposition. If <5% change, report as robust to design reduction.\n148\t\n149\t### MD9. Title-Content Alignment\n150\t**Issue**: Title promises \"Evaluation Metrics\" but logprob analysis is minimal.\n151\t**Defense**: Expand logprob analysis across all benchmarks in exp-004, OR adjust title to \"...Across Benchmarks and Models.\"\n152\t\n153\t## Required Bibliography (minimum 25 references)\n154\tKey references to include beyond current literature_map:\n155\t- Bouthillier et al. (2021, JMLR) — ML variance decomposition via ANOVA\n156\t- Lalor et al. (2019, NAACL) — IRT-based item analysis in NLP\n157\t- Rodriguez et al. (2021, ACL) — IRT evaluation for NLP\n158\t- Polo et al. (2024) — tinyBenchmarks, IRT-based subsampling\n159\t- Perlitz et al. (2023) — Efficient LLM benchmarking\n160\t- Henderson et al. (2018) — Deep RL reproducibility, variance decomposition\n161\t- Shavelson & Webb (1991) — G-theory primer (canonical)\n162\t- Brennan (2001) — G-theory textbook (canonical, Ch.12 on binary data)\n163\t- Lu et al. (2022) — Option ordering effects in MC evaluation\n164\t- Pezeshkpour & Hruschka (2024) — Option ordering effects\n165\t- Dodge et al. (2020, EMNLP) — Fine-tuning instability (seed, data order)\n166\t- Sellam et al. (2022) — MultiBERTs, multi-seed training\n167\t- Dehghani et al. (2021) — Benchmark Lottery\n168\t- Biderman et al. (2024) — Evaluation reproducibility lessons\n169\t- Vania et al. (2021) — IRT item properties in NLP\n170\t- **Sclar et al. (2024, ICLR)** — Up to 76% accuracy swing from prompt formatting; directly relevant to prompt×item variance [Dream #3: all 3 tiers flagged as missing]\n171\t- **Kunievsky & Evans (2025)** — Alternative decomposition: intent/articulation/model uncertainty; competing framework to G-theory [Dream #3: all 3 tiers flagged]\n172\t- **Bui et al. (2025)** — Extreme seed sensitivity in fine-tuning (RTES variance=18.22 for RoBERTa on COPA) [Dream #3: relevant to exp-006]\n173\t- **Jiang & Skorupski (2017)** — Logistic-link generalized G-theory for binary data [Dream #3 worst: methodological defense for REML on binary]\n174\t- **Mushquash & O'Connor (2006)** — Binary-data G-theory [Dream #3: classical psychometrics gap]\n175\t- **Shavelson et al. (1989)** — Binary response G-theory treatment [Dream #3: classical psychometrics]\n176\t\n177\t## Dream #3 Framing Directives (2026-05-21, D006)\n178\tPost-analysis framing adjustments based on three-tier consistent feedback:\n179\t1. **Center gradient + model×item as primary contributions** — infrastructure crossing is confirmatory secondary\n180\t2. **Position explicitly as empirical extension of Mesting (2026)** with novel findings (model×item quantification, D-study out-of-sample validation, IRT comparison), not a new framework. Remove \"first\" claims where not defensible.\n181\t3. **Scope Claim 2 to \"precision separability\"** — not \"infrastructure\" broadly. Single GPU architecture.\n182\t4. **Item dominance is psychometrically expected** — contribution is quantification of magnitude, metric-conditionality, and D-study implications\n183\t5. **Fine-tuning (exp-006) downgraded** to Discussion-section preliminary observation, not a numbered contribution\n184\t6. **Add explicit judge-facet exclusion discussion** vs Mesting: we fix judge to isolate other factors; Mesting's 43.8% judge variance would dominate\n185\t7. **Practitioner toolkit must be concrete**: specify released scripts, D-study calculator, budget lookup tables\n186\t8. **Figures are non-negotiable**: 5 planned figures address all-tier \"zero figures\" criticism\n187\t\n188\t## Dream #4 Framing Directives (2026-05-21, D008)\n189\tPost-analysis adjustments based on Dream #4 (best 7, median 6, worst 5):\n190\t1. **NEW — Format confound in gradient** [三档一致]: MC (MMLU, ARC) vs free-form (GSM8K, MATH) vs completion (HellaSwag) format differences may partially drive gradient. Defense: (a) within-format gradient analysis (MMLU→ARC within MC; GSM8K→MATH within free-form) when exp-002 data available; (b) explicit Discussion paragraph on format as potential confound; (c) if within-format gradient preserved, format is not the driver.\n191\t2. **D-study code documentation**: Released code must include joint optimization (not just single-facet sweeps). Fix before submission.\n192\t3. **Bibliography**: Add CAT (Computerized Adaptive Testing) and MTMM (Multi-Trait Multi-Method) references per median tier.\n193\t4. **Data consistency audit**: Ensure all numbers in paper match analysis outputs exactly — multiple tiers flagged inconsistencies between code/text (residual values, record counts, LOMO spread). Pre-submission checklist item.\n194\t5. **Sclar et al. discrepancy**: Our prompt×item is 3.8-4.6% vs Sclar's 76% accuracy swing. Explain: Sclar measures max-min across adversarial formatting; we measure variance across representative templates. Different questions, compatible answers.\n195\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"fd78be70-01f0-4e4d-915a-b0c15db3a431","uuid":"a784ebc6-5fa2-481b-85b1-c6e171e2360b","timestamp":"2026-05-21T13:12:11.790Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0519/projects/nlp_variance_gtheory/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260521T122449/best/paper_framing.md","content":"# Paper Framing — nlp_variance_gtheory\n\n## Target Venue\n- Primary: AAAI 2027 (abstract Jul 21, paper Jul 28 — 9 weeks from today)\n- Backup: ACL 2027 via ARR (October 15 cycle — 21 weeks)\n- Extended: TACL (rolling)\n\n## Title\nReproducibility Budgets for LLM Evaluation: A Generalizability Theory Analysis of Variance Sources Across Benchmarks, Models, and Evaluation Metrics\n\n## Core Claims (3 条，可证伪)\n\n**Claim 1 — Model-Conditional Item Dominance**: Item selection is the single largest variance source in single-model LLM evaluation (61.1% on MMLU), but model×item interaction (26.2%) emerges as the second-largest source when model is treated as a crossed facet. Item difficulty rankings are model-specific: two models with similar aggregate accuracy (Llama 66.4% vs Gemma 73.2%) disagree dramatically at the item level — item_id drops from 61% (single-model) to 38% (cross-model) as model×item absorbs the model-conditional portion. This means single-model variance decompositions overestimate item dominance by conflating universal and model-specific item difficulty. Cross-benchmark gradient (pending exp-002) is hypothesized to further modulate this structure.\n**Empirical anchors (exp-001, 4 models, balanced bf16, 345,600 records)**: Single-model item_id=61.1%, cross-model item_id=42.1% [36.0-48.8%] (↓19pp), model×item=33.6% [28.4-37.9%] (second-largest source, increased from 3-model 31.7%). G_item=0.820 [0.782-0.861]. D-study prediction validated out-of-sample: 3-model predicted G=0.816 for n=4, actual=0.820 (Δ=0.004). Forward projection: 5 models → G=0.848, 8→0.893, 10→0.910 (G≥0.90 requires ~10 models). Residual dropped 24%→19.5% as 4th model split residual into model×item. Per-model interaction: Mistral 29.25% (lowest accuracy 63.8%) > Qwen 27.11% > Gemma 23.95% > Llama 19.69%.\n**REML robustness**: REML-EM (500 iter, practical convergence Δ<0.003pp) confirms Henderson I: all components differ <0.5pp. Henderson I unbiased for balanced designs. Dataset B differences (item_id 38→52%, model×item 26→12%) due to design differences (factor selection), not estimator bias — measurement design choices themselves affect observed variance structure.\n**Completed robustness**: BF16-vs-FP16 sensitivity confirms precision→0.0006% without FP32 (FP32 is sole source of precision variance); leave-one-model-out (matched-spec) shows G_item range 0.747-0.793 across 3-model subsets (spread=0.046) — all below 0.80, confirming 4 models is the minimum for reliable cross-model measurement. LOMO also confirms item_id dominance and model×item as second-largest source are stable across all subsets.\n**GLMM cross-check (dual-analysis, must report both)**: Two complementary analyses:\n(i) *Marginal logit ANOVA*: All 7 grouped component rankings preserved exactly (ρ=1.0), consistent with MQL-1 prediction that logit-scaling is approximately constant at p̄=0.70.\n(ii) *VB GLMM (25-item subsample)*: Ranking reversal on top-2 — model:item_id=47.3% > item_id=22.8% on logit scale (ρ=0.571, p=0.18). Attributable to model underspecification (missing 4 item-interaction terms in VB model) + subsample.\n**Paper must present BOTH honestly** — \"marginal logit ANOVA preserves all rankings; VB GLMM shows differences attributable to model underspecification.\" Do NOT overwrite ρ=0.571 with ρ=1.0. Implication: model×item interaction may be even more important than Henderson I indicates on logit scale — strengthens Claim 1's core message.\n\n**Claim 2 — Conditional Separability**: Infrastructure factors (numerical precision) and methodology factors (prompt, seed, temperature, ordering) are approximately separable at the aggregate level — direct infrastructure×methodology interaction ≈0% under binary scoring; the only detectable cross-factor interaction (precision×temperature=1.3%) emerges under text-exact-match scoring, consistent with floating-point non-associativity (Messina & Scotta, 2026). Both factor types interact substantially with item difficulty (precision×item, prompt×item), making their effects item-conditional. This provides a metric-qualified confirmatory finding for isolated-factor studies while warning that conclusions are conditional on item selection and evaluation metric. Note: with only numerical precision tested on a single GPU architecture (pending exp-003 for hardware crossing), \"infrastructure\" is currently limited to the precision facet.\n\n**Claim 3 — Metric-Conditional Reproducibility Budget**: Reproducibility budgets are fundamentally metric-dependent and temperature-conditional. Binary correct: G=0.611 (200 items), G≥0.80 requires ~510 items (all facets random) or ~414 items (practical: precision+temperature fixed). Text-exact-match: G=0.007 (temperature dominates at 79.7%), unreliable at any temperature >0. Temperature-stratified analysis reveals binary G degrades monotonically (T=0.0: 0.760 → T=0.7: 0.549). G-theory requires 3.6-4.4× more items than IRT (510 vs 116) because it accounts for prompt×item (4.6%), seed×item (4.7%), and residual (21.6%) dimensions that IRT ignores. Current practices (1 prompt, 1 seed, ≤200 items) yield G=0.611 — below the 0.80 threshold for dependable measurement.\n\n## Contributions (4 条，明确区分)\n\n1. **Model×item interaction as the hidden dominant source**: In a balanced 4-model design (345,600 records), model×item interaction (33.6%, CI [28.4-37.9%]) is the second-largest variance source, while item_id drops from 61% (single-model) to 42%. Item difficulty rankings are model-specific — single-model G-studies overestimate item dominance by conflating universal and model-specific difficulty. G_item=0.820 [0.782-0.861] with 4 models; D-study prediction validated out-of-sample (3-model predicted 0.816, actual 0.820, Δ=0.004). Reaching G≥0.90 requires ~10 models. [Note: position as quantification of a phenomenon expected from measurement theory, not as a surprising discovery. The novelty is the magnitude, the out-of-sample D-study validation, and the cross-model generalizability implications.]\n\n2. **Precision separability with metric qualification**: Precision×methodology interactions are negligible under binary scoring (<0.1%), providing empirical support for isolated-factor studies. Under text-exact-match scoring, precision×temperature reaches 4.15%, consistent with floating-point non-associativity. Separability is confirmed as metric-conditional. [Note: scope to \"numerical precision on single GPU architecture\" — not \"infrastructure\" broadly. This is a confirmatory secondary finding, not a primary contribution. Pending BF16-vs-FP16 sensitivity to isolate FP32/vLLM confound.]\n\n3. **IRT vs G-theory: the cost of multi-facet reliability**: The first empirical quantification of the multi-facet reliability cost for LLM evaluation: G-theory requires 3.6-4.4× more items than IRT (510 vs 116 for MMLU) because it accounts for prompt, seed, and ordering dimensions that IRT ignores. At IRT's recommended 116 items, G-theory G is only 0.529. This quantifies the \"hidden cost\" of evaluation dimensions invisible to IRT. [Note: keep as numbered contribution — Dream + Reviewer consensus this is one of the paper's strongest findings. Wording adjusted from \"first direct comparison\" to \"first empirical quantification\" per SF6 resolution.]\n\n4. **Practitioner toolkit**: Open-source G-theory variance decomposition pipeline + D-study budget calculator for LLM evaluation. Includes: (a) benchmark-specific reproducibility budget tables (items × prompts × seeds for target G), (b) temperature-stratified reliability guide, (c) model-as-facet analysis template. Addresses \"purely diagnostic\" criticism — provides actionable tools, not just warnings.\n\n## Methodological Robustness Package (6 验证分析)\nAll completed on Llama 239,200 records:\n- **Binary data not artifact**: Null simulation item_id=0.08% vs observed 61.1% (737.5×); obs/Bernoulli ratio=1.0008\n- **Fixed vs random insensitive**: Binary G ranges 0.611-0.664 across all facet classification choices\n- **Bootstrap = jackknife**: CI width ratio 0.96-1.04, independence violation negligible\n- **Temperature stratification**: Binary G degrades T=0.0 (0.760) → T=0.7 (0.549); text metric unreliable at all T>0\n- **D-study formula verified**: Monotonicity assertion added; min_items_G80=414 (practical) / 510 (all random)\n- **IRT comparison**: G-theory 3.6-4.4× more conservative than IRT; difference attributable to prompt/seed/ordering dimensions\n- **REML vs Henderson I**: REML-EM (500 iter) confirms all components within <0.5pp of Henderson I; Henderson I unbiased for balanced designs\n- **BF16-vs-FP16 sensitivity**: Excluding FP32, precision effect drops from 0.4% to 0.0006% (precision×item: 1.3%→0.04%). FP32 anomaly is entirely a vLLM enforce_eager artifact, not a fundamental precision effect. BF16≈FP16 (Δ=0.23pp accuracy)\n- **Leave-one-model-out stability**: All 3-model subsets preserve item_id dominance (39-45%) and model×item as second-largest (32-37%). G_item range 0.747-0.793 (spread=0.046), all below 0.80 — confirming 4 models is the minimum configuration for G≥0.80. This strengthens the D-study \"need ~10 models for G≥0.90\" prediction.\n\n## Story Spine\n\n**Problem**: When LLM evaluation results vary across reproductions, practitioners cannot determine whether the culprit is prompt phrasing, random seed, GPU hardware, numerical precision, temperature, or item selection — let alone how these factors interact. Every prior study isolates at most two sources, implicitly assuming separability.\n\n**Existing approaches & gaps**: Bouthillier et al. (2021) decomposed ML benchmark variance across hyperparameters, data splits, and seeds via ANOVA, establishing the template for factorial variance decomposition in ML evaluation. Mesting (2026) adapted G-theory to 5 methodological facets in LLM evaluation but treated infrastructure as fixed. Yuan et al. (2025) documented up to 9% accuracy variation from hardware/precision without factorial attribution. Brittlebench (2026) quantified prompt sensitivity at 50% variance on fixed hardware. IRT-based approaches (Lalor et al. 2019; Rodriguez et al. 2021; Polo et al. 2024) have analyzed item-level properties and efficient subsampling, but from a different methodological perspective than G-theory. No study has: (a) crossed infrastructure and methodology in a unified G-theory framework, (b) compared variance structure across multiple benchmarks, or (c) provided metric-conditional reproducibility budgets.\n\n**Our framing**: We conduct a multi-benchmark G-theory study crossing infrastructure (numerical precision) × methodology (prompt, seed, temperature, ordering) × measurement (item) factors, with model treated as a crossed facet. The infrastructure crossing yields a confirmatory null result: aggregate separability holds, validating the implicit assumption of prior isolated-factor studies. The primary novel finding is the cross-benchmark variance gradient — item dominance systematically weakens as tasks shift from knowledge retrieval to multi-step reasoning — explaining divergent conclusions in the literature. D-study projections provide the first benchmark-specific, metric-conditional reproducibility budgets for LLM evaluation, directly comparable to IRT-based subsampling approaches.\n\n**Headline result template**: \"In a G-study of 345,600 evaluation records across 4 models on MMLU (200 items × 6 prompts × 6 seeds × 3 temperatures × 4 orderings), item selection accounts for 42% of variance when model is treated as a crossed facet — but model×item interaction (34%) emerges as the second-largest source, revealing that item difficulty rankings are substantially model-specific (G_item=0.82, requiring ~10 models for G≥0.90). A D-study from 3 models predicted the 4-model G coefficient within 0.004 — out-of-sample validation of the D-study methodology. G-theory requires 3.6-4.4× more items than IRT for reliable measurement because it accounts for prompt, seed, and ordering dimensions that IRT ignores. Numerical precision×methodology interactions are negligible under binary scoring (<0.1%). We release an open-source G-theory pipeline and D-study budget calculator.\"\n\n## Hook (Counter-Intuitive Quantified Claim)\n\"Researchers debating whether to standardize prompts, seeds, or GPU hardware for LLM evaluation implicitly assume that their findings will generalize across benchmarks — our G-theory analysis across N benchmarks shows the variance structure itself is benchmark-conditional, rendering any 'universal reproducibility recipe' illusory.\"\n\n## Narrative Strategy\n- **Type**: Cross-domain transfer (psychometrics G-theory → ML evaluation) + Empirical contribution (benchmark-conditional gradient)\n- **Tension**: \"No universal recipe\" — the field seeks standardization but variance structure varies qualitatively across task types\n- **Intro structure**: 4 paragraphs + 3 contributions (field mode from distilled writing patterns)\n- **Signature visual**: Stacked-bar variance decomposition chart across benchmarks (field idiom from Mesting/Brittlebench)\n- **Figure plan** (5 figures):\n  1. **Stacked-bar: single-model vs cross-model variance decomposition** — item_id shrinks from 61% to 42%, model×item appears at 34%, residual drops 24%→20%. The \"reveal\" figure.\n  2. **G-theory crossed design diagram** — facets, levels, crossing structure\n  3. **D-study projection curves** — G vs item count for binary (practical vs all-random) and text metric. Shows G≥0.80 threshold at ~414/510 items.\n  4. **IRT vs G-theory item requirement comparison** — bar chart showing 116 (IRT) vs 510 (G-theory) items, with breakdown of where the extra items go (prompt×item, seed×item, residual)\n  5. **Temperature-stratified G coefficient heatmap** — binary vs text metric × 3 temperatures, showing metric×temperature interaction\n- **Key framing shifts from Dream feedback**:\n  - Infrastructure crossing framed as **confirmatory** (validates isolated-factor studies), not as primary novelty\n  - Item dominance acknowledged as **psychometrically expected**; contribution is quantification + benchmark gradient\n  - Cross-benchmark gradient is the **primary novel finding**\n  - \"Fully crossed\" language qualified to MMLU exp-001 only; other benchmarks use reduced designs\n\n## Venue Fit Risks\n- AAAI: Strong fit for \"evaluation methodology\" track. Risk: tight 9-week timeline requires overlapping experiments with writing.\n- Reviewer concern 1: \"Only open-weight models, not API models\" — We intentionally study open-weight models where infrastructure is controllable; API models (studied by Mesting) have opaque infrastructure. Frame as complementary.\n- Reviewer concern 2: \"G-theory is borrowed methodology, not novel\" — Frame as cross-domain transfer. The novelty is the cross-benchmark variance gradient finding, not G-theory itself.\n- Reviewer concern 3: \"Item dominance is obvious from measurement theory\" — Acknowledge explicitly. Contribution is quantification (X-Y%, pending exp-001/002), benchmark-dependence, and D-study implications. Contextualize against IRT item analysis literature.\n\n## Anticipated Reviewer Concerns & Planned Responses\n1. **\"Only N GPU types\"** → exp-003 (multi-hardware crossing) addresses this. If blocked, explicitly scope to \"numerical precision\" and frame hardware as future work.\n2. **\"float32 engine confound\"** → exp-001 fixes with V0 enforce_eager for all precisions. Confound discovery itself is a standalone methodological contribution.\n3. **\"Only MMLU\"** → exp-002 expands to GSM8K, ARC-Challenge, HellaSwag. Cross-benchmark gradient is the core finding.\n4. **\"Small item sample\"** → exp-001 expands from 50 to 200 items. exp-001b tests resampling stability.\n5. **\"No training-time analysis\"** → exp-006 (fine-tuning G-study with RoBERTa). Explicitly qualify as encoder-only classification; decoder fine-tuning is future work.\n6. **\"Missing scale validation\"** → exp-005 (14B models)\n7. **\"G-theory may not be right framework\"** → Compare with ANOVA and regression decomposition as sensitivity analysis. Cite Bouthillier et al. (2021) as ANOVA-based predecessor; show G-theory adds D-study projections.\n8. **\"Separability claim is metric-dependent\"** → This is precisely the point: reproducibility budgets must be metric-qualified. Replace arbitrary threshold with bootstrap CI on interaction term.\n9. **\"FP32 degradation is already known (Yuan 2025)\"** → Position as supporting finding with novel item-level breakdown. Not an independent claim.\n10. **\"Incremental over Mesting (2026)\"** → Infrastructure crossing is confirmatory (valuable as validation, not breakthrough). Primary novelty: cross-benchmark gradient, task-specific budgets, dual-metric analysis, confound fix. Mesting = single benchmark, no infrastructure; we = multi-benchmark, infrastructure confirmation + benchmark gradient.\n11. **\"Model not treated as G-theory facet\"** → exp-004 includes model-as-facet reanalysis of MMLU data. Report model×item interaction separately from residual.\n12. **\"No CIs on variance components\"** → exp-004 includes bootstrap CIs (1000 resamples) on all variance components in all tables.\n13. **\"Missing related work — IRT, tinyBenchmarks, Bouthillier\"** → Comprehensive bibliography (25+ refs). Position D-study budgets against IRT-based subsampling. Cite Bouthillier et al. as ANOVA predecessor.\n14. **\"Diagnostic tool claim unsubstantiated\"** → Release analysis pipeline code (G-theory variance decomposition + D-study projections) as reusable tool. Or reframe as \"empirical analysis + data release.\"\n15. **\"Fine-tuning uses encoder-only RoBERTa\"** → Explicitly qualify. Decoder fine-tuning is a natural extension requiring substantially more compute.\n\n## Design Decisions\n- **Judge model fixed**: Unlike Mesting who includes judge as a facet, we fix the judge to isolate infrastructure × methodology interactions. Judge variance (Mesting: 43.8%) would dominate and obscure the effects we study. Adding judge is a natural extension for future work.\n- **Generation-based evaluation**: Following Hua et al. (EMNLP 2025), we avoid log-likelihood scoring to eliminate scoring artifacts that inflate apparent prompt sensitivity.\n- **Default vLLM settings**: We use default vLLM settings (without batch-invariant kernels) to reflect realistic practitioner conditions, making CUDA-level nondeterminism a measurable variance source.\n- **vLLM 0.9.2**: Required for Blackwell GPU (sm_100/sm_120) + CUDA 12.8 support.\n- **Model as crossed quasi-random facet**: In exp-001 analysis, model is treated as a crossed quasi-random facet — purposive sample from 4 major architecture families (Llama, Gemma, Mistral, Qwen), not a random draw from all LLMs. Paper must explicitly state selection criteria, discuss purposive vs random sampling implications, and present model-as-fixed sensitivity analysis. D-study Δ=0.004 out-of-sample prediction supports exchangeability assumption. Plan to add 2-3 models (Yi-1.5-9B, OLMo-2-7B, etc.) to strengthen to 6-7 models per MF2 resolution.\n- **Reduced designs for non-MMLU benchmarks**: exp-002 uses 2 precisions × 2 temperatures (vs 3×3 in exp-001) due to GPU budget constraints. Explicitly described as \"reduced crossing\" rather than \"fully crossed.\"\n- **Gemma2 float16 exclusion**: Gemma2 architecture does not support float16 inference (vLLM numerical instability guard). Cross-model analysis uses bf16+fp32 (balanced). fp16 data from Llama/Mistral/Qwen used in within-model analyses. bf16≈fp16 difference <0.1pp (D005).\n\n## Methodological Defenses (Dream #2 审稿问题逐条应对)\n\n### MD1. Binary Data in G-theory\n**Issue**: Standard G-theory assumes continuous, approximately normal response variables. Binary 0/1 data violates this; item variance is mechanically linked to item difficulty via Bernoulli p(1-p).\n**Defense strategy (upgraded per Round 1 MF1 + Reviewer + GLMM results)**: Four-line defense, reordered by strength:\n(a) **PRIMARY — Simulation-based recovery** ✅ DONE (matched design verified): Probit latent model → binary, 100 replications × {50,100,200} items × {0.5-0.8} accuracy. **Strongest finding (matched 1,296-condition design): binary ρ=0.732 ≈ continuous ρ=0.741 at n=200 (Δ=0.009) — binary binarization adds NO ranking distortion beyond what continuous data already has.** Henderson I identifies item_id as #1 systematic source in 99-100% of runs. Top-3/top-5 accuracy weaker (55-76% / 58-82%) — do NOT claim \"ranking recovery equivalent\", instead: \"robustly identifies dominant source; smaller component ordering comparable between binary and continuous.\" **Paper must discuss**: (i) residual inflation (latent 16%→~40%, Bernoulli within-cell variance); (ii) \"no-residual\" Spearman is correct metric; (iii) CI coverage slightly anti-conservative at high accuracy (86.4% at p=0.8).\n(b) **COMPLEMENTARY — GLMM logistic-link**: GLMM (Jiang & Skorupski 2017) on 25-item MMLU subsample showed **ranking reversal**: model:item_id #1 (47.3%) > item_id #2 (22.8%) on logit scale, vs Henderson I item_id #1 (42.1%) > model:item_id #2 (33.6%). Spearman ρ=0.571, p=0.18 — rankings NOT statistically consistent. BUT: macro-level agreement holds (item-related >> condition effects in both). Reversal is theoretically expected — logit link amplifies DIF (differential item functioning) for extreme-difficulty items. **Paper framing**: \"Henderson I and GLMM agree on macro structure; differ on item_id vs model:item_id ordering due to scale properties of logit link. This is a complementary perspective, not a contradiction. GLMM suggests model:item interaction may be even more dominant than Henderson I indicates.\" Do NOT claim GLMM \"validates\" Henderson I.\n(c) **Bernoulli-adjusted decomposition**: ✅ DONE. Percentage decomposition inherently normalized by p(1-p) ceiling. Per-model adjustment preserved interaction gradient (Mistral rank drops from #1→#3 but interaction still substantial). Cross-accuracy resampling (0.62-0.72) shows item_id% stable (r=-0.048 with accuracy).\n(d) **Logprob robustness check**: Continuous metric analysis showing gradient preserved — bypasses binary assumption entirely.\n(e) **Methods subsection**: \"Binary Data Assumptions\" discussing Henderson I bias properties for balanced designs, citing Brennan Ch.12, Shavelson et al. 1989, Mushquash & O'Connor 2006, Lalor et al. 2019 (IRT on binary NLP data). Must note GLMM residual drops to 0.004% as Bernoulli variance absorbed by link function.\n\n### MD2. Fixed vs Random Facet Sensitivity\n**Issue**: Temperature (2-3 design points) and ordering (4 systematic rotations) are not randomly sampled from a universe — treating them as random facets may be inappropriate.\n**Defense strategy**: Run sensitivity analysis with temperature and ordering as fixed facets. If variance decomposition changes <5%, report as robust. If substantial, discuss implications and present both analyses. This directly addresses Reviewer concern.\n\n### MD3. Separability Claim Scope\n**Issue**: Separability (<0.3%) holds for knowledge benchmarks but not reasoning tasks (GSM8K=3.0%, MATH=2.5%).\n**Defense**: Qualify all separability claims as \"under binary scoring on knowledge-retrieval benchmarks.\" Present benchmark-conditional separability as a finding (not a limitation): \"separability itself varies across task types.\"\n\n### MD4. D-study Optimization Documentation\n**Issue**: Optimization procedure undocumented; Table 10 arithmetic anomaly (fewer items → higher G).\n**Defense**: Document grid search procedure (bounds, step sizes, objective function). Verify and explain any non-monotonicity (likely due to joint optimization across facets where reallocating item budget to prompt/seed is more efficient).\n\n### MD5. G-coefficient Interpretation\n**Issue**: G=0.22 for 200 items ≠ \"MMLU is unreliable\" (standard MMLU uses ~14K items).\n**Defense**: (a) Clearly distinguish \"G for our 200-item subsample design\" from \"reliability of full MMLU\"; (b) Report D-study projection to 14K items; (c) Frame G=0.22 as \"cost of subsampling\" rather than \"MMLU is broken.\"\n\n### MD6. Bootstrap Independence\n**Issue**: Item-level bootstrap with replacement creates ~127 unique items per 200-item resample, violating independence.\n**Defense**: (a) Implement delete-1 jackknife as comparison; (b) If bootstrap and jackknife CIs are similar (within 20%), report as robust; (c) Discuss limitation in Methods.\n\n### MD7. IRT vs G-theory Comparison\n**Issue**: tinyBenchmarks recommends ~100 items, D-study recommends 420. 4× discrepancy unexplained.\n**Defense**: Direct comparison showing G-theory accounts for prompt/seed/ordering dimensions that IRT ignores. The 4× difference is the \"cost of multi-facet reliability\" — G-theory's budget is larger because it targets reliability across ALL facets, not just items. This comparison is a potential standalone contribution.\n\n### MD8. Cross-benchmark Design Asymmetry\n**Issue**: MMLU uses 3 prec × 3 temp, other benchmarks use 2 × 2.\n**Defense**: Re-analyze MMLU with matched 2×2 design (bf16/fp16 × T=0.0/T=0.7) and compare variance decomposition. If <5% change, report as robust to design reduction.\n\n### MD9. Title-Content Alignment\n**Issue**: Title promises \"Evaluation Metrics\" but logprob analysis is minimal.\n**Defense**: Expand logprob analysis across all benchmarks in exp-004, OR adjust title to \"...Across Benchmarks and Models.\"\n\n## Required Bibliography (minimum 25 references)\nKey references to include beyond current literature_map:\n- Bouthillier et al. (2021, JMLR) — ML variance decomposition via ANOVA\n- Lalor et al. (2019, NAACL) — IRT-based item analysis in NLP\n- Rodriguez et al. (2021, ACL) — IRT evaluation for NLP\n- Polo et al. (2024) — tinyBenchmarks, IRT-based subsampling\n- Perlitz et al. (2023) — Efficient LLM benchmarking\n- Henderson et al. (2018) — Deep RL reproducibility, variance decomposition\n- Shavelson & Webb (1991) — G-theory primer (canonical)\n- Brennan (2001) — G-theory textbook (canonical, Ch.12 on binary data)\n- Lu et al. (2022) — Option ordering effects in MC evaluation\n- Pezeshkpour & Hruschka (2024) — Option ordering effects\n- Dodge et al. (2020, EMNLP) — Fine-tuning instability (seed, data order)\n- Sellam et al. (2022) — MultiBERTs, multi-seed training\n- Dehghani et al. (2021) — Benchmark Lottery\n- Biderman et al. (2024) — Evaluation reproducibility lessons\n- Vania et al. (2021) — IRT item properties in NLP\n- **Sclar et al. (2024, ICLR)** — Up to 76% accuracy swing from prompt formatting; directly relevant to prompt×item variance [Dream #3: all 3 tiers flagged as missing]\n- **Kunievsky & Evans (2025)** — Alternative decomposition: intent/articulation/model uncertainty; competing framework to G-theory [Dream #3: all 3 tiers flagged]\n- **Bui et al. (2025)** — Extreme seed sensitivity in fine-tuning (RTES variance=18.22 for RoBERTa on COPA) [Dream #3: relevant to exp-006]\n- **Jiang & Skorupski (2017)** — Logistic-link generalized G-theory for binary data [Dream #3 worst: methodological defense for REML on binary]\n- **Mushquash & O'Connor (2006)** — Binary-data G-theory [Dream #3: classical psychometrics gap]\n- **Shavelson et al. (1989)** — Binary response G-theory treatment [Dream #3: classical psychometrics]\n\n## Dream #3 Framing Directives (2026-05-21, D006)\nPost-analysis framing adjustments based on three-tier consistent feedback:\n1. **Center gradient + model×item as primary contributions** — infrastructure crossing is confirmatory secondary\n2. **Position explicitly as empirical extension of Mesting (2026)** with novel findings (model×item quantification, D-study out-of-sample validation, IRT comparison), not a new framework. Remove \"first\" claims where not defensible.\n3. **Scope Claim 2 to \"precision separability\"** — not \"infrastructure\" broadly. Single GPU architecture.\n4. **Item dominance is psychometrically expected** — contribution is quantification of magnitude, metric-conditionality, and D-study implications\n5. **Fine-tuning (exp-006) downgraded** to Discussion-section preliminary observation, not a numbered contribution\n6. **Add explicit judge-facet exclusion discussion** vs Mesting: we fix judge to isolate other factors; Mesting's 43.8% judge variance would dominate\n7. **Practitioner toolkit must be concrete**: specify released scripts, D-study calculator, budget lookup tables\n8. **Figures are non-negotiable**: 5 planned figures address all-tier \"zero figures\" criticism\n\n## Dream #4 Framing Directives (2026-05-21, D008)\nPost-analysis adjustments based on Dream #4 (best 7, median 6, worst 5):\n1. **NEW — Format confound in gradient** [三档一致]: MC (MMLU, ARC) vs free-form (GSM8K, MATH) vs completion (HellaSwag) format differences may partially drive gradient. Defense: (a) within-format gradient analysis (MMLU→ARC within MC; GSM8K→MATH within free-form) when exp-002 data available; (b) explicit Discussion paragraph on format as potential confound; (c) if within-format gradient preserved, format is not the driver.\n2. **D-study code documentation**: Released code must include joint optimization (not just single-facet sweeps). Fix before submission.\n3. **Bibliography**: Add CAT (Computerized Adaptive Testing) and MTMM (Multi-Trait Multi-Method) references per median tier.\n4. **Data consistency audit**: Ensure all numbers in paper match analysis outputs exactly — multiple tiers flagged inconsistencies between code/text (residual values, record counts, LOMO spread). Pre-submission checklist item.\n5. **Sclar et al. discrepancy**: Our prompt×item is 3.8-4.6% vs Sclar's 76% accuracy swing. Explain: Sclar measures max-min across adversarial formatting; we measure variance across representative templates. Different questions, compatible answers.\n","numLines":195,"startLine":1,"totalLines":195}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01LwqFq3kMRJiRekYtJZvk3d","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t[\n2\t  {\n3\t    \"name\": \"Multi-benchmark replication (Phase 1-3 complete, Phase 4 partial)\",\n4\t    \"description\": \"8 models × 5 benchmarks × BF16 × 2 temps × 6 prompts × 6 seeds × 4 orderings × 200 items. Phase 1: 4 original models × 4 benchmarks (MMLU/ARC/HellaSwag/GSM8K) on westd-32570. Phase 2: + MATH as 5th benchmark (all models). Phase 3: + Yi-1.5-9B, OLMo-2-7B on all 5 benchmarks (westd-15695). Phase 4 (partial): + InternLM2.5-7B started, DeepSeek-7B not yet launched.\\n\",\n5\t    \"results\": {\n6\t      \"phase_1_status\": \"DONE — 4 models × 4 benchmarks complete\",\n7\t      \"phase_2_status\": \"DONE — MATH added for all 4 original + 2 new models\",\n8\t      \"phase_3_status\": \"DONE — Yi-1.5-9B and OLMo-2-7B complete on all 5 benchmarks\",\n9\t      \"phase_4_status\": \"PARTIAL — InternLM2.5-7B on 3/5 benchmarks; DeepSeek-7B not started\",\n10\t      \"n_models_completed\": 7,\n11\t      \"n_benchmarks_completed\": 5,\n12\t      \"total_records\": 2016000,\n13\t      \"item_id_pct_MMLU\": 42.1,\n14\t      \"item_id_pct_ARC\": 35.8,\n15\t      \"item_id_pct_HellaSwag\": 27.4,\n16\t      \"item_id_pct_GSM8K\": 19.6,\n17\t      \"item_id_pct_MATH\": 14.2,\n18\t      \"model_x_item_pct_MMLU\": 33.6,\n19\t      \"model_x_item_pct_ARC\": 37.2,\n20\t      \"model_x_item_pct_HellaSwag\": 41.8,\n21\t      \"model_x_item_pct_GSM8K\": 48.3,\n22\t      \"model_x_item_pct_MATH\": 53.7,\n23\t      \"gradient_spread_item_id_pp\": 27.9,\n24\t      \"gradient_monotonicity_perm_p\": 0.008,\n25\t      \"gradient_direction\": \"monotonically decreasing from knowledge-retrieval to multi-step reasoning\",\n26\t      \"within_MC_gradient_MMLU_vs_ARC\": \"42.1 → 35.8 (−6.3pp within MC format)\",\n27\t      \"within_freeform_gradient_GSM8K_vs_MATH\": \"19.6 → 14.2 (−5.4pp within free-form)\",\n28\t      \"within_format_gradient_preserved\": true,\n29\t      \"HellaSwag_below_ARC\": true,\n30\t      \"G_item_MMLU\": 0.82,\n31\t      \"G_item_ARC\": 0.776,\n32\t      \"G_item_HellaSwag\": 0.714,\n33\t      \"G_item_GSM8K\": 0.653,\n34\t      \"G_item_MATH\": 0.612,\n35\t      \"D_study_items_G80_MMLU\": 340,\n36\t      \"D_study_items_G80_ARC\": 420,\n37\t      \"D_study_items_G80_HellaSwag\": 510,\n38\t      \"D_study_items_G80_GSM8K\": 660,\n39\t      \"D_study_items_G80_MATH\": 780,\n40\t      \"precision_x_methodology_all_benchmarks\": \"<0.8%\",\n41\t      \"precision_separability_MMLU\": 0.04,\n42\t      \"precision_separability_ARC\": 0.02,\n43\t      \"precision_separability_HellaSwag\": 0.06,\n44\t      \"precision_separability_GSM8K\": 0.12,\n45\t      \"precision_separability_MATH\": 0.09,\n46\t      \"accuracy_GSM8K_Qwen3_8B\": 0.782,\n47\t      \"accuracy_GSM8K_Llama\": 0.571,\n48\t      \"accuracy_GSM8K_Gemma\": 0.624,\n49\t      \"accuracy_GSM8K_Mistral\": 0.488,\n50\t      \"accuracy_GSM8K_Yi\": 0.653,\n51\t      \"accuracy_GSM8K_OLMo\": 0.412,\n52\t      \"accuracy_GSM8K_InternLM\": 0.697,\n53\t      \"residual_pct_MMLU\": 19.5,\n54\t      \"residual_pct_ARC\": 21.3,\n55\t      \"residual_pct_HellaSwag\": 24.6,\n56\t      \"residual_pct_GSM8K\": 26.8,\n57\t      \"residual_pct_MATH\": 27.4\n58\t    },\n59\t    \"tables\": [\n60\t      {\n61\t        \"name\": \"Table 3: Cross-Benchmark Variance Decomposition (Henderson I, 7 models × BF16)\",\n62\t        \"columns\": [\n63\t          \"Component\",\n64\t          \"MMLU\",\n65\t          \"ARC\",\n66\t          \"HellaSwag\",\n67\t          \"GSM8K\",\n68\t          \"MATH\"\n69\t        ],\n70\t        \"rows\": [\n71\t          [\n72\t            \"item_id (%)\",\n73\t            42.1,\n74\t            35.8,\n75\t            27.4,\n76\t            19.6,\n77\t            14.2\n78\t          ],\n79\t          [\n80\t            \"model×item (%)\",\n81\t            33.6,\n82\t            37.2,\n83\t            41.8,\n84\t            48.3,\n85\t            53.7\n86\t          ],\n87\t          [\n88\t            \"model (%)\",\n89\t            0.5,\n90\t            0.8,\n91\t            1.2,\n92\t            2.4,\n93\t            3.1\n94\t          ],\n95\t          [\n96\t            \"prompt×item (%)\",\n97\t            2.8,\n98\t            2.6,\n99\t            2.9,\n100\t            1.4,\n101\t            0.8\n102\t          ],\n103\t          [\n104\t            \"seed×item (%)\",\n105\t            2.5,\n106\t            2.1,\n107\t            1.8,\n108\t            1.2,\n109\t            0.6\n110\t          ],\n111\t          [\n112\t            \"ordering×item (%)\",\n113\t            0.6,\n114\t            0.4,\n115\t            0.3,\n116\t            0.1,\n117\t            0.1\n118\t          ],\n119\t          [\n120\t            \"residual (%)\",\n121\t            19.5,\n122\t            21.3,\n123\t            24.6,\n124\t            26.8,\n125\t            27.4\n126\t          ],\n127\t          [\n128\t            \"G_item\",\n129\t            0.82,\n130\t            0.776,\n131\t            0.714,\n132\t            0.653,\n133\t            0.612\n134\t          ],\n135\t          [\n136\t            \"D-study items (G≥0.80)\",\n137\t            340,\n138\t            420,\n139\t            510,\n140\t            660,\n141\t            780\n142\t          ]\n143\t        ]\n144\t      },\n145\t      {\n146\t        \"name\": \"Table 4: Gradient Monotonicity Test\",\n147\t        \"columns\": [\n148\t          \"Test\",\n149\t          \"Statistic\",\n150\t          \"p-value\",\n151\t          \"Conclusion\"\n152\t        ],\n153\t        \"rows\": [\n154\t          [\n155\t            \"Permutation test (item_id% gradient)\",\n156\t            \"τ = −1.0 (perfect monotone)\",\n157\t            0.008,\n158\t            \"Reject H₀ at α=0.01\"\n159\t          ],\n160\t          [\n161\t            \"Within-MC (MMLU→ARC)\",\n162\t            \"Δ = −6.3pp\",\n163\t            0.023,\n164\t            \"Significant within format\"\n165\t          ],\n166\t          [\n167\t            \"Within-freeform (GSM8K→MATH)\",\n168\t            \"Δ = −5.4pp\",\n169\t            0.031,\n170\t            \"Significant within format\"\n171\t          ]\n172\t        ]\n173\t      }\n174\t    ],\n175\t    \"figures\": [],\n176\t    \"analysis\": \"exp-002 Phase 1-3 完成的核心贡献是实证验证了 cross-benchmark variance gradient 的存在和单调性。 7 个模型 × 5 个基准的设计提供了足够的统计功效（Phase 4 的缺失不影响主结论）。 三个关键发现支撑论文叙事：(1) item_id% 从 42%→14% 的单调梯度，排列检验 p=0.008； (2) within-format gradient 排除格式混淆作为主驱动力；(3) D-study 预算在基准间变化 2.3 倍 （340→780 items），直接产出实用的 benchmark-specific 信度指南。 Phase 4 缺失 DeepSeek-7B 和 InternLM 部分数据不影响梯度结论——leave-one-model-out 分析 显示去掉任一模型后梯度方向和显著性不变（所有子集 p<0.05）。\\n\\n\\n知识检索型基准（MMLU、ARC）的 item 难度在模型间相对一致——大多数模型要么\\\"知道\\\"某个事实要么不知道， 因此 item_id 主效应大（42%、36%），model×item 交互相对低（34%、37%）。随着任务从知识检索转向 多步推理（HellaSwag→GSM8K→MATH），正确率越来越依赖模型特有的推理策略和 chain-of-thought 能力， 而非共享的知识基础。不同模型在同一道推理题上的成败模式高度分化——Qwen3 擅长代数变换但 Mistral 擅长 几何推理——导致 model×item 交互急剧上升（48%→54%），item_id 主效应相应压缩（20%→14%）。 这在 G-theory 框架下表现为：推理基准的 item 难度排序更加 model-conditional，G_item 系数下降 （0.82→0.61），D-study 需要更多 item 才能达到同等信度（340→780）。\\nwithin-format gradient 得以保持的原因：即使在同为 MC 格式的 MMLU 和 ARC 中，ARC 的 item 涉及更多科学推理和因果判断（比 MMLU 的知识回忆要求更高），因此 item_id 下降 6pp。同理， MATH 比 GSM8K 需要更复杂的多步代数/几何推理，item_id 再下降 5pp。这说明梯度的主驱动力是 认知复杂度而非答题格式。HellaSwag（completion format）的 item_id 低于 ARC（MC format） 但高于 GSM8K，也符合其任务特性——需要常识推理但不需要数学符号操作。\\n残差从 MMLU 的 19.5% 上升到 MATH 的 27.4%，反映推理型基准中更多的随机性来源—— 生成式回答的格式变异、chain-of-thought 的路径依赖性、以及 temperature 采样在长序列上的 累积效应都增加了不可归因的变异。\\n梯度单调性排列检验 p=0.008 (5000次排列)，在 α=0.01 水平显著，这是因为 7 个模型 × 5 个基准 提供了足够的统计功效，且效应量（27.9pp spread）远超偶然波动。\\n\"\n177\t  },\n178\t  {\n179\t    \"name\": \"Multi-hardware G-study\",\n180\t    \"description\": \"4 models × 2+ GPU types × 2 precisions (BF16, FP16) × 6 prompts × 6 seeds × 3 temperatures × 4 orderings × 200 MMLU items. Tests hardware × methodology interaction.\\n\",\n181\t    \"results\": {\n182\t      \"status\": \"planned — not executed within AAAI timeline\",\n183\t      \"paper_treatment\": \"Explicitly scoped as future work; Claim 2 narrowed to 'precision separability'\",\n184\t      \"rationale_for_skip\": \"Second GPU type not available; Claim 2 already defensible with precision-only scope\",\n185\t      \"hypothetical_hardware_x_methodology_binary\": 0.15,\n186\t      \"hypothetical_hardware_x_item\": 0.8,\n187\t      \"hypothetical_hardware_main_effect\": 0.3,\n188\t      \"hypothetical_total_hardware_variance\": 1.25\n189\t    },\n190\t    \"tables\": [],\n191\t    \"figures\": [],\n192\t    \"analysis\": \"exp-003 在 best 档中属于 \\\"nice-to-have\\\" 而非 \\\"必须完成\\\"。论文通过三重防御策略处理其缺失： (1) 标题和正文中 \\\"infrastructure\\\" 全部替换为 \\\"numerical precision\\\"； (2) Discussion 中明确指出硬件交叉是自然延伸方向； (3) exp-001 的 BF16≈FP16 (Δ=0.23pp) 结果本身已表明在同一 GPU 上精度不是主要变异源。\\n\\n\\n在 best 档下 exp-003 未执行，因为第二种 GPU 类型未到位。但这不影响论文投稿—— Claim 2 已从 \\\"infrastructure separability\\\" 缩窄为 \\\"precision separability\\\"（D003/D007 决议）， 仅基于 exp-001 的 BF16/FP16/FP32 交叉即可支撑。BF16-vs-FP16 灵敏度分析（排除 FP32 后 precision effect 从 0.4% 降至 0.0006%）已证明在单一 GPU 架构上精度几乎不影响二值评分结果。 审稿人可能追问 \\\"hardware≠precision\\\"，但论文已在 MD3 中预备了应对——明确说明 scope 限制并将 hardware crossing 列为 future work。如果假设执行了，预期硬件主效应 ~0.3%， hardware×item ~0.8%（不同 GPU 的数值调度差异在 item 层面累积）， hardware×methodology <0.2%——与精度分离性一致。\\n\"\n193\t  },\n194\t  {\n195\t    \"name\": \"Comprehensive analysis package\",\n196\t    \"description\": \"对 exp-001 + exp-001b + exp-002 (Phase 1-3) 数据的全面分析。包含 17 个子分析项： model-as-facet 再分析、bootstrap CI、D-study 投影、REML 比较、logprob 评分、 排名稳定性、高阶交互、IRT 对比、统计功效、固定/随机灵敏度、二值数据分析、 bootstrap/jackknife 比较、设计不对称灵敏度、全尺寸 G 投影、GPU 成本分析。\\n\",\n197\t    \"results\": {\n198\t      \"cross_model_item_id_range\": \"14.2% (MATH) — 42.1% (MMLU)\",\n199\t      \"cross_model_model_x_item_range\": \"33.6% (MMLU) — 53.7% (MATH)\",\n200\t      \"single_vs_cross_model_item_id_drop_MMLU\": 19.0,\n201\t      \"single_vs_cross_model_item_id_drop_MATH\": 38.8,\n202\t      \"bootstrap_CI_item_id_MMLU\": [\n203\t        38.0,\n204\t        46.8\n205\t      ],\n206\t      \"bootstrap_CI_model_x_item_MMLU\": [\n207\t        28.4,\n208\t        37.9\n209\t      ],\n210\t      \"bootstrap_CI_item_id_MATH\": [\n211\t        10.1,\n212\t        18.6\n213\t      ],\n214\t      \"bootstrap_CI_model_x_item_MATH\": [\n215\t        47.8,\n216\t        59.1\n217\t      ],\n218\t      \"CI_width_item_id_mean\": 8.2,\n219\t      \"CI_width_model_x_item_mean\": 10.4,\n220\t      \"all_CIs_non_overlapping_for_top2\": true,\n221\t      \"D_study_items_G80_practical_MMLU\": 340,\n222\t      \"D_study_items_G80_practical_MATH\": 780,\n223\t      \"D_study_items_G80_all_random_MMLU\": 510,\n224\t      \"D_study_items_G80_all_random_MATH\": 1040,\n225\t      \"D_study_items_G90_MMLU_7models\": 620,\n226\t      \"D_study_prediction_validation\": \"4-model G predicted from 3-model: Δ=0.004 (MMLU); 7-model predicted from 4-model: Δ<0.01 (cross-validated)\",\n227\t      \"REML_Henderson_max_diff_pp\": 0.47,\n228\t      \"REML_Henderson_rank_correlation\": 1.0,\n229\t      \"REML_convergence\": \"500 iterations, Δ<0.003pp final step\",\n230\t      \"logprob_item_id_MMLU\": 51.3,\n231\t      \"logprob_item_id_ARC\": 44.7,\n232\t      \"logprob_model_x_item_MMLU\": 28.4,\n233\t      \"logprob_gradient_preserved\": true,\n234\t      \"logprob_gradient_monotonicity_p\": 0.012,\n235\t      \"model_ranking_stable_across_conditions\": true,\n236\t      \"kendall_tau_model_rank_MMLU\": 0.94,\n237\t      \"precision_x_temp_alters_ranking\": false,\n238\t      \"three_way_interactions_total\": 2.8,\n239\t      \"residual_after_three_way\": 16.7,\n240\t      \"largest_three_way\": \"model×prompt×item = 1.2%\",\n241\t      \"IRT_recommended_items_MMLU\": 116,\n242\t      \"Gtheory_recommended_items_MMLU\": 510,\n243\t      \"ratio_G_to_IRT_MMLU\": 4.4,\n244\t      \"IRT_recommended_items_GSM8K\": 84,\n245\t      \"Gtheory_recommended_items_GSM8K\": 660,\n246\t      \"ratio_G_to_IRT_GSM8K\": 7.9,\n247\t      \"at_IRT_items_G_coefficient_MMLU\": 0.529,\n248\t      \"at_IRT_items_G_coefficient_GSM8K\": 0.387,\n249\t      \"IRT_ignores_dimensions\": \"prompt×item (2.8%), seed×item (2.5%), ordering×item (0.6%), model×item (33.6%)\",\n250\t      \"power_to_detect_5pct_interaction\": 0.99,\n251\t      \"power_to_detect_1pct_interaction\": 0.94,\n252\t      \"minimum_detectable_effect_80pct_power\": 0.6,\n253\t      \"practitioner_guide_format\": \"Lookup table: benchmark × target_G → (items, prompts, seeds)\",\n254\t      \"temperature_recommendation\": \"Fix T=0.0 for reproducibility (saves ~15% item budget)\",\n255\t      \"fixed_facet_G_range\": [\n256\t        0.611,\n257\t        0.664\n258\t      ],\n259\t      \"fixed_random_max_diff\": 5.3,\n260\t      \"fixed_random_conclusion\": \"Robust — top-2 component ordering unchanged\",\n261\t      \"bernoulli_p1p_correlation_with_item_var\": 0.34,\n262\t      \"null_simulation_item_id\": 0.08,\n263\t      \"obs_to_bernoulli_ratio\": 1.0008,\n264\t      \"CI_width_ratio_boot_jack\": [\n265\t        0.96,\n266\t        1.04\n267\t      ],\n268\t      \"conclusion_bootstrap_valid\": true,\n269\t      \"MMLU_3x3_vs_2x2_item_id_diff\": 1.2,\n270\t      \"MMLU_3x3_vs_2x2_model_x_item_diff\": 0.8,\n271\t      \"design_reduction_robust\": true,\n272\t      \"G_14K_items_MMLU_all_random\": 0.987,\n273\t      \"G_14K_items_MMLU_practical\": 0.992,\n274\t      \"logprob_coverage\": \"5/5 benchmarks\",\n275\t      \"supports_title_evaluation_metrics\": true,\n276\t      \"cost_per_Gstudy_single_benchmark\": \"~$12-18 (consumer GPU rental)\",\n277\t      \"cost_recommended_Dstudy_budget\": \"$50-120 per benchmark depending on target G\"\n278\t    },\n279\t    \"tables\": [\n280\t      {\n281\t        \"name\": \"Table 5: IRT vs G-theory Item Requirements Across Benchmarks\",\n282\t        \"columns\": [\n283\t          \"Benchmark\",\n284\t          \"IRT items (G≥0.80)\",\n285\t          \"G-theory items (G≥0.80, all random)\",\n286\t          \"Ratio\",\n287\t          \"G at IRT items\"\n288\t        ],\n289\t        \"rows\": [\n290\t          [\n291\t            \"MMLU\",\n292\t            116,\n293\t            510,\n294\t            \"4.4×\",\n295\t            0.529\n296\t          ],\n297\t          [\n298\t            \"ARC\",\n299\t            98,\n300\t            420,\n301\t            \"4.3×\",\n302\t            0.512\n303\t          ],\n304\t          [\n305\t            \"HellaSwag\",\n306\t            105,\n307\t            510,\n308\t            \"4.9×\",\n309\t            0.478\n310\t          ],\n311\t          [\n312\t            \"GSM8K\",\n313\t            84,\n314\t            660,\n315\t            \"7.9×\",\n316\t            0.387\n317\t          ],\n318\t          [\n319\t            \"MATH\",\n320\t            72,\n321\t            1040,\n322\t            \"14.4×\",\n323\t            0.312\n324\t          ]\n325\t        ]\n326\t      },\n327\t      {\n328\t        \"name\": \"Table 6: Bootstrap 95% CIs on Top-2 Variance Components\",\n329\t        \"columns\": [\n330\t          \"Benchmark\",\n331\t          \"item_id% [CI]\",\n332\t          \"model×item% [CI]\",\n333\t          \"Dominant source\"\n334\t        ],\n335\t        \"rows\": [\n336\t          [\n337\t            \"MMLU\",\n338\t            \"42.1 [38.0, 46.8]\",\n339\t            \"33.6 [28.4, 37.9]\",\n340\t            \"item_id\"\n341\t          ],\n342\t          [\n343\t            \"ARC\",\n344\t            \"35.8 [31.2, 40.7]\",\n345\t            \"37.2 [32.8, 42.1]\",\n346\t            \"≈tied\"\n347\t          ],\n348\t          [\n349\t            \"HellaSwag\",\n350\t            \"27.4 [23.1, 32.0]\",\n351\t            \"41.8 [36.5, 47.4]\",\n352\t            \"model×item\"\n353\t          ],\n354\t          [\n355\t            \"GSM8K\",\n356\t            \"19.6 [15.4, 24.1]\",\n357\t            \"48.3 [42.6, 53.8]\",\n358\t            \"model×item\"\n359\t          ],\n360\t          [\n361\t            \"MATH\",\n362\t            \"14.2 [10.1, 18.6]\",\n363\t            \"53.7 [47.8, 59.1]\",\n364\t            \"model×item\"\n365\t          ]\n366\t        ]\n367\t      },\n368\t      {\n369\t        \"name\": \"Table 7: D-study Budget Lookup (Target G × Benchmark)\",\n370\t        \"columns\": [\n371\t          \"Target G\",\n372\t          \"MMLU\",\n373\t          \"ARC\",\n374\t          \"HellaSwag\",\n375\t          \"GSM8K\",\n376\t          \"MATH\"\n377\t        ],\n378\t        \"rows\": [\n379\t          [\n380\t            \"G ≥ 0.80\",\n381\t            \"340 items\",\n382\t            \"420 items\",\n383\t            \"510 items\",\n384\t            \"660 items\",\n385\t            \"780 items\"\n386\t          ],\n387\t          [\n388\t            \"G ≥ 0.90\",\n389\t            \"620 items\",\n390\t            \"780 items\",\n391\t            \"940 items\",\n392\t            \"1200 items\",\n393\t            \"1450 items\"\n394\t          ],\n395\t          [\n396\t            \"G ≥ 0.95\",\n397\t            \"1050 items\",\n398\t            \"1300 items\",\n399\t            \"1580 items\",\n400\t            \"2020 items\",\n401\t            \"2400 items\"\n402\t          ]\n403\t        ]\n404\t      },\n405\t      {\n406\t        \"name\": \"Table S1: Methodological Robustness Summary (17 analyses)\",\n407\t        \"columns\": [\n408\t          \"Analysis\",\n409\t          \"Conclusion\",\n410\t          \"Key number\"\n411\t        ],\n412\t        \"rows\": [\n413\t          [\n414\t            \"REML vs Henderson I\",\n415\t            \"Concordant\",\n416\t            \"max Δ = 0.47pp, rank ρ = 1.0\"\n417\t          ],\n418\t          [\n419\t            \"Fixed vs random facets\",\n420\t            \"Robust\",\n421\t            \"G range 0.611–0.664\"\n422\t          ],\n423\t          [\n424\t            \"Bootstrap vs jackknife CI\",\n425\t            \"Concordant\",\n426\t            \"width ratio 0.96–1.04\"\n427\t          ],\n428\t          [\n429\t            \"Null simulation (binary)\",\n430\t            \"Not artifact\",\n431\t            \"null item_id = 0.08% vs obs 42%\"\n432\t          ],\n433\t          [\n434\t            \"Design reduction (3×3→2×2)\",\n435\t            \"Robust\",\n436\t            \"item_id Δ = 1.2pp\"\n437\t          ],\n438\t          [\n439\t            \"Power (1% interaction)\",\n440\t            \"Adequate\",\n441\t            \"power = 0.94\"\n442\t          ],\n443\t          [\n444\t            \"Logprob gradient\",\n445\t            \"Preserved\",\n446\t            \"monotonicity p = 0.012\"\n447\t          ]\n448\t        ]\n449\t      }\n450\t    ],\n451\t    \"figures\": [],\n452\t    \"analysis\": \"exp-004 将 exp-001 和 exp-002 的原始数据转化为可发表的证据包。最重要的输出是： (1) Table 6 的 CI 交叉——MMLU 上 item_id 显著 > model×item，MATH 上显著反转， HellaSwag/ARC 是交叉点（CI 重叠），这为 \\\"benchmark-conditional variance hierarchy\\\" 提供了严格的统计证据； (2) Table 5 的 IRT 对比——这是论文最可引用的单一结果，直接量化了忽略多面向变异的代价； (3) 17 项灵敏度分析全部返回 \\\"robust/concordant\\\"，构成完整的方法学防御链， 预封堵了 Dream #2 识别的所有审稿问题。\\n\\n\\nexp-004 是纯分析实验（GPU hours = 0），在 best 档下全部 17 个子项均完成。核心发现：\\n(a-b) Bootstrap CI 表明 item_id 和 model×item 在所有基准上的 95% 区间不重叠—— MMLU 上 item_id [38.0, 46.8] vs model×item [28.4, 37.9]，MATH 上则反转： item_id [10.1, 18.6] vs model×item [47.8, 59.1]。这为 \\\"反转\\\" 提供了统计证据， 不仅仅是点估计的差异。CI 宽度 ~8-10pp 反映了 200 item 设计在 bootstrap 下的合理不确定性。\\n(c) D-study预测的 out-of-sample 验证是方法学亮点——3→4 模型预测误差仅 0.004， 4→7 模型预测误差 <0.01，证明 D-study 投影在此设计下可靠。\\n(d) REML 与 Henderson I 的一致性（<0.5pp, rank ρ=1.0）源于设计的近似平衡性—— Henderson I 在平衡设计下是无偏的（Brennan Ch.3），而我们的设计仅因 Gemma FP16 缺失而 轻微不平衡，REML 的迭代校正效果极小。\\n(e) Logprob 连续指标上梯度同样单调（p=0.012），且 item_id% 普遍高于 binary（~+9pp）， 因为连续评分保留了更多 item 间难度差异信息。这支撑标题中的 \\\"Evaluation Metrics\\\" 声明。\\n(h) IRT vs G-theory 的比较是论文最强贡献之一。IRT 推荐 116 items（MMLU），此时 G-theory 的 G 仅 0.529——远低于 0.80 阈值。差异来源清晰：IRT 不考虑 prompt×item (2.8%)、 seed×item (2.5%) 和 model×item (33.6%) 维度。在 GSM8K 上这个差距更大（7.9×）， 因为推理基准的 model×item 交互更强，IRT 忽略的变异更多。\\n(i) 功效分析确认设计有 >99% 的功效检测 5% 交互效应，94% 检测 1% 效应—— 远超审稿人 \\\"underpowered\\\" 的担忧。这得益于 ~2M 条记录的大样本量。\\n(k-n) 四项灵敏度分析均返回 \\\"robust\\\" 结论，为方法学防御提供了完整证据链。\\n\"\n453\t  },\n454\t  {\n455\t    \"name\": \"14B model scale extension\",\n456\t    \"description\": \"Qwen3-14B 和 DeepSeek-R1-Distill-14B 在数据中心 GPU 上运行 BF16/FP16， 测试方差归因模式是否随模型规模和架构变化。\\n\",\n457\t    \"results\": {\n458\t      \"status\": \"planned — not executed within AAAI timeline\",\n459\t      \"paper_treatment\": \"Listed as future work in Discussion; brief mention in Limitations\",\n460\t      \"hypothetical_item_id_14B_MMLU\": 39.5,\n461\t      \"hypothetical_model_x_item_14B\": 35.8,\n462\t      \"hypothetical_gradient_preserved_at_14B\": true,\n463\t      \"hypothetical_accuracy_improvement\": \"+4-8pp over 7-9B counterparts\"\n464\t    },\n465\t    \"tables\": [],\n466\t    \"figures\": [],\n467\t    \"analysis\": \"不影响 AAAI 投稿。Reviewer concern #6 (\\\"Missing scale validation\\\") 的应对策略是 指出 7-9B 是当前 reproducibility 研究最相关的规模段（多数评估管线和 tinyBenchmarks 文献均在此规模），14B+ 是自然延伸但不影响核心 claims 的有效性。\\n\\n\\nexp-005 在 best 档下未执行（需要 ≥48GB VRAM 的 GPU 且依赖 exp-003）。 但这对论文影响有限——7-9B 参数量级已覆盖了当前开源社区最常用的模型尺寸段。 如果执行，预期方差结构定性不变但定量微调：14B 模型的更高 accuracy 会略微压缩 item_id% （更多 items 被所有模型答对，item 间变异减小），同时 model×item 因更强的特异化推理能力 而略增。梯度方向（知识→推理）不会因模型规模改变——这是任务类型的固有属性。 论文在 Discussion 中注明 \\\"variance structure at 14B+ scale is a natural extension that may reveal scale-conditional effects, particularly for reasoning benchmarks where larger models show qualitatively different solution strategies.\\\"\\n\"\n468\t  },\n469\t  {\n470\t    \"name\": \"Fine-tuning G-study (RoBERTa)\",\n471\t    \"description\": \"RoBERTa-large 在 SST-2/MNLI/RTE/COPA 上的 fine-tuning G-study。 6 seeds × 3 learning rates × 4 data shuffles × 3 GPUs × 2 precisions = 432 runs/task × 4 tasks。\\n\",\n472\t    \"results\": {\n473\t      \"status\": \"planned — not executed within AAAI timeline\",\n474\t      \"paper_treatment\": \"Downgraded to Discussion-section preliminary observation per D006 directive\",\n475\t      \"hypothetical_dominant_source_finetuning\": \"seed (35-45%)\",\n476\t      \"hypothetical_item_id_finetuning\": \"15-25%\",\n477\t      \"hypothetical_lr_x_seed\": \"8-12%\",\n478\t      \"hypothetical_data_shuffle_effect\": \"5-8%\",\n479\t      \"hypothetical_precision_effect\": \"<1%\",\n480\t      \"hypothetical_contrast_with_inference\": \"Fine-tuning: seed > item > lr×seed > data_shuffle. Inference: item > model×item > residual > prompt×item. Completely different variance hierarchies.\\n\"\n481\t    },\n482\t    \"tables\": [],\n483\t    \"figures\": [],\n484\t    \"analysis\": \"明智地降级为 Discussion。encoder-only RoBERTa 的 fine-tuning 与 decoder LLM 的 inference 差异太大，混在同一篇论文中会分散叙事焦点。留作后续独立研究更合适。\\n\\n\\nexp-006 在 best 档下未执行，且按 D006 指令已降级为 Discussion 的初步展望。 如果执行，fine-tuning 的方差层级预计与 inference 根本不同：训练随机种子通过 参数初始化和 mini-batch 采样顺序对最终模型产生级联影响，尤其在小数据集（RTE 2.5K、 COPA 400 训练样本）上，seed 效应可达 35-45%（Bui et al. 2025 报告 RoBERTa 在 COPA 上 RTES variance=18.22）。Item 难度仍重要但被 seed 效应压缩到 15-25%。 Learning rate × seed 交互 (8-12%) 反映不同 lr 在不同初始化下的训练动力学差异—— 小 lr 对种子更稳定，大 lr 放大种子敏感性。精度效应在 fine-tuning 中同样微小 (<1%)， 因为梯度累积的数值差异在数千步训练中被 SGD 的随机性淹没。\\n论文仅在 Discussion 中提一句：\\\"Preliminary fine-tuning analysis (encoder-only,  not included in main results) suggests a qualitatively different variance hierarchy  where random seed dominates item selection — an expected reversal given the  stochastic nature of gradient-based optimization.\\\"\\n\"\n485\t  },\n486\t  {\n487\t    \"name\": \"LayerCast precision mitigation\",\n488\t    \"description\": \"测试 LayerCast (FP16 storage + FP32 compute) 作为额外精度条件在 MMLU 上的效果。\\n\",\n489\t    \"results\": {\n490\t      \"status\": \"planned — not executed within AAAI timeline\",\n491\t      \"paper_treatment\": \"Not mentioned in paper\",\n492\t      \"hypothetical_layercast_accuracy\": \"~0.1pp above FP16, ~0.1pp below FP32_V0\",\n493\t      \"hypothetical_layercast_variance_reduction\": \"precision×item drops from 1.98% to ~0.3%\",\n494\t      \"hypothetical_practical_value\": \"Minimal — BF16≈FP16 already shows precision is negligible\"\n495\t    },\n496\t    \"tables\": [],\n497\t    \"figures\": [],\n498\t    \"analysis\": \"P2 优先级，不进入 AAAI 版本。即使执行，贡献也是增量性的—— 证实了一种具体的精度缓解策略有效，但不改变任何方差结构结论。\\n\\n\\nLayerCast 在 best 档下未执行，且不影响任何 claim。exp-001 已证明排除 FP32 后 BF16≈FP16 (Δ=0.23pp)，精度对二值评分结果的影响可忽略。LayerCast 作为一种 \\\"混合精度推理\\\" 策略理论上能进一步缩小精度差异，但当差异已经 <0.3pp 时， 这种缩小没有实际意义。如果执行，LayerCast 的 accuracy 会落在 FP16 和 FP32 之间， 因为它在计算关键层使用 FP32 精度但存储使用 FP16——减少了 FP32 enforce_eager 带来的 vLLM 引擎差异。precision×item 从 1.98% 降至 ~0.3% 反映混合精度 消除了纯 FP32 的系统性偏差但保留了极少量的层间精度传播差异。\\n\"\n499\t  },\n500\t  {\n501\t    \"name\": \"MATH benchmark (merged into exp-002)\",\n502\t    \"description\": \"MATH benchmark 200 items。已合并入 exp-002 作为第 5 个 benchmark，不再是独立实验。\\n\",\n503\t    \"results\": {\n504\t      \"status\": \"merged into exp-002 Phase 2\",\n505\t      \"independent_status\": \"N/A\",\n506\t      \"see_also\": \"exp-002 expected results for MATH-specific numbers\",\n507\t      \"MATH_item_id_pct\": 14.2,\n508\t      \"MATH_model_x_item_pct\": 53.7,\n509\t      \"MATH_G_item\": 0.612,\n510\t      \"MATH_D_study_items_G80\": 780,\n511\t      \"MATH_overall_accuracy\": 0.318,\n512\t      \"MATH_accuracy_range_across_models\": \"0.11 (OLMo) — 0.52 (Qwen3)\"\n513\t    },\n514\t    \"tables\": [],\n515\t    \"figures\": [],\n516\t    \"analysis\": \"MATH 数据完全在 exp-002 中收集和报告。作为梯度终点，它对论文叙事至关重要—— 没有 MATH，gradient spread 仅为 ~22pp (MMLU→GSM8K)，加入后扩展到 ~28pp， 增强了效应量和排列检验的显著性。\\n\\nMATH 作为 exp-002 的第 5 个基准，是梯度最极端的端点。item_id 降至 14.2% 而 model×item 升至 53.7% 的原因在于 MATH 题目的解题路径高度多样——同一道代数证明题，不同模型可能 走完全不同的推导链（符号化简 vs 数值代入 vs 图形论证），成败模式几乎完全是 model-specific 的。 这也解释了模型间 accuracy 的极端分散（0.11-0.52）——MATH 是 7-9B 参数模型能力分化 最剧烈的基准。G_item=0.612 意味着 200 道 MATH 题在 7 个模型间的 item 难度一致性 远低于信度阈值——需要 780 道题才能达到 G≥0.80，是 MMLU 的 2.3 倍。 这直接产出实用指导：\\\"评估 LLM 的数学推理能力需要比知识检索多一倍以上的题目。\\\"\\n\"\n517\t  }\n518\t]"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"fd78be70-01f0-4e4d-915a-b0c15db3a431","uuid":"f33edd99-349e-4994-8c01-3b923b12ad92","timestamp":"2026-05-21T13:12:11.971Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0519/projects/nlp_variance_gtheory/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260521T122449/best/experiment_data.json","content":"[\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Multi-benchmark replication (Phase 1-3 complete, Phase 4 partial)\",\n    \"description\": \"8 models × 5 benchmarks × BF16 × 2 temps × 6 prompts × 6 seeds × 4 orderings × 200 items. Phase 1: 4 original models × 4 benchmarks (MMLU/ARC/HellaSwag/GSM8K) on westd-32570. Phase 2: + MATH as 5th benchmark (all models). Phase 3: + Yi-1.5-9B, OLMo-2-7B on all 5 benchmarks (westd-15695). Phase 4 (partial): + InternLM2.5-7B started, DeepSeek-7B not yet launched.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"phase_1_status\": \"DONE — 4 models × 4 benchmarks complete\",\n      \"phase_2_status\": \"DONE — MATH added for all 4 original + 2 new models\",\n      \"phase_3_status\": \"DONE — Yi-1.5-9B and OLMo-2-7B complete on all 5 benchmarks\",\n      \"phase_4_status\": \"PARTIAL — InternLM2.5-7B on 3/5 benchmarks; DeepSeek-7B not started\",\n      \"n_models_completed\": 7,\n      \"n_benchmarks_completed\": 5,\n      \"total_records\": 2016000,\n      \"item_id_pct_MMLU\": 42.1,\n      \"item_id_pct_ARC\": 35.8,\n      \"item_id_pct_HellaSwag\": 27.4,\n      \"item_id_pct_GSM8K\": 19.6,\n      \"item_id_pct_MATH\": 14.2,\n      \"model_x_item_pct_MMLU\": 33.6,\n      \"model_x_item_pct_ARC\": 37.2,\n      \"model_x_item_pct_HellaSwag\": 41.8,\n      \"model_x_item_pct_GSM8K\": 48.3,\n      \"model_x_item_pct_MATH\": 53.7,\n      \"gradient_spread_item_id_pp\": 27.9,\n      \"gradient_monotonicity_perm_p\": 0.008,\n      \"gradient_direction\": \"monotonically decreasing from knowledge-retrieval to multi-step reasoning\",\n      \"within_MC_gradient_MMLU_vs_ARC\": \"42.1 → 35.8 (−6.3pp within MC format)\",\n      \"within_freeform_gradient_GSM8K_vs_MATH\": \"19.6 → 14.2 (−5.4pp within free-form)\",\n      \"within_format_gradient_preserved\": true,\n      \"HellaSwag_below_ARC\": true,\n      \"G_item_MMLU\": 0.82,\n      \"G_item_ARC\": 0.776,\n      \"G_item_HellaSwag\": 0.714,\n      \"G_item_GSM8K\": 0.653,\n      \"G_item_MATH\": 0.612,\n      \"D_study_items_G80_MMLU\": 340,\n      \"D_study_items_G80_ARC\": 420,\n      \"D_study_items_G80_HellaSwag\": 510,\n      \"D_study_items_G80_GSM8K\": 660,\n      \"D_study_items_G80_MATH\": 780,\n      \"precision_x_methodology_all_benchmarks\": \"<0.8%\",\n      \"precision_separability_MMLU\": 0.04,\n      \"precision_separability_ARC\": 0.02,\n      \"precision_separability_HellaSwag\": 0.06,\n      \"precision_separability_GSM8K\": 0.12,\n      \"precision_separability_MATH\": 0.09,\n      \"accuracy_GSM8K_Qwen3_8B\": 0.782,\n      \"accuracy_GSM8K_Llama\": 0.571,\n      \"accuracy_GSM8K_Gemma\": 0.624,\n      \"accuracy_GSM8K_Mistral\": 0.488,\n      \"accuracy_GSM8K_Yi\": 0.653,\n      \"accuracy_GSM8K_OLMo\": 0.412,\n      \"accuracy_GSM8K_InternLM\": 0.697,\n      \"residual_pct_MMLU\": 19.5,\n      \"residual_pct_ARC\": 21.3,\n      \"residual_pct_HellaSwag\": 24.6,\n      \"residual_pct_GSM8K\": 26.8,\n      \"residual_pct_MATH\": 27.4\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 3: Cross-Benchmark Variance Decomposition (Henderson I, 7 models × BF16)\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Component\",\n          \"MMLU\",\n          \"ARC\",\n          \"HellaSwag\",\n          \"GSM8K\",\n          \"MATH\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"item_id (%)\",\n            42.1,\n            35.8,\n            27.4,\n            19.6,\n            14.2\n          ],\n          [\n            \"model×item (%)\",\n            33.6,\n            37.2,\n            41.8,\n            48.3,\n            53.7\n          ],\n          [\n            \"model (%)\",\n            0.5,\n            0.8,\n            1.2,\n            2.4,\n            3.1\n          ],\n          [\n            \"prompt×item (%)\",\n            2.8,\n            2.6,\n            2.9,\n            1.4,\n            0.8\n          ],\n          [\n            \"seed×item (%)\",\n            2.5,\n            2.1,\n            1.8,\n            1.2,\n            0.6\n          ],\n          [\n            \"ordering×item (%)\",\n            0.6,\n            0.4,\n            0.3,\n            0.1,\n            0.1\n          ],\n          [\n            \"residual (%)\",\n            19.5,\n            21.3,\n            24.6,\n            26.8,\n            27.4\n          ],\n          [\n            \"G_item\",\n            0.82,\n            0.776,\n            0.714,\n            0.653,\n            0.612\n          ],\n          [\n            \"D-study items (G≥0.80)\",\n            340,\n            420,\n            510,\n            660,\n            780\n          ]\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 4: Gradient Monotonicity Test\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Test\",\n          \"Statistic\",\n          \"p-value\",\n          \"Conclusion\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"Permutation test (item_id% gradient)\",\n            \"τ = −1.0 (perfect monotone)\",\n            0.008,\n            \"Reject H₀ at α=0.01\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Within-MC (MMLU→ARC)\",\n            \"Δ = −6.3pp\",\n            0.023,\n            \"Significant within format\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Within-freeform (GSM8K→MATH)\",\n            \"Δ = −5.4pp\",\n            0.031,\n            \"Significant within format\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"exp-002 Phase 1-3 完成的核心贡献是实证验证了 cross-benchmark variance gradient 的存在和单调性。 7 个模型 × 5 个基准的设计提供了足够的统计功效（Phase 4 的缺失不影响主结论）。 三个关键发现支撑论文叙事：(1) item_id% 从 42%→14% 的单调梯度，排列检验 p=0.008； (2) within-format gradient 排除格式混淆作为主驱动力；(3) D-study 预算在基准间变化 2.3 倍 （340→780 items），直接产出实用的 benchmark-specific 信度指南。 Phase 4 缺失 DeepSeek-7B 和 InternLM 部分数据不影响梯度结论——leave-one-model-out 分析 显示去掉任一模型后梯度方向和显著性不变（所有子集 p<0.05）。\\n\\n\\n知识检索型基准（MMLU、ARC）的 item 难度在模型间相对一致——大多数模型要么\\\"知道\\\"某个事实要么不知道， 因此 item_id 主效应大（42%、36%），model×item 交互相对低（34%、37%）。随着任务从知识检索转向 多步推理（HellaSwag→GSM8K→MATH），正确率越来越依赖模型特有的推理策略和 chain-of-thought 能力， 而非共享的知识基础。不同模型在同一道推理题上的成败模式高度分化——Qwen3 擅长代数变换但 Mistral 擅长 几何推理——导致 model×item 交互急剧上升（48%→54%），item_id 主效应相应压缩（20%→14%）。 这在 G-theory 框架下表现为：推理基准的 item 难度排序更加 model-conditional，G_item 系数下降 （0.82→0.61），D-study 需要更多 item 才能达到同等信度（340→780）。\\nwithin-format gradient 得以保持的原因：即使在同为 MC 格式的 MMLU 和 ARC 中，ARC 的 item 涉及更多科学推理和因果判断（比 MMLU 的知识回忆要求更高），因此 item_id 下降 6pp。同理， MATH 比 GSM8K 需要更复杂的多步代数/几何推理，item_id 再下降 5pp。这说明梯度的主驱动力是 认知复杂度而非答题格式。HellaSwag（completion format）的 item_id 低于 ARC（MC format） 但高于 GSM8K，也符合其任务特性——需要常识推理但不需要数学符号操作。\\n残差从 MMLU 的 19.5% 上升到 MATH 的 27.4%，反映推理型基准中更多的随机性来源—— 生成式回答的格式变异、chain-of-thought 的路径依赖性、以及 temperature 采样在长序列上的 累积效应都增加了不可归因的变异。\\n梯度单调性排列检验 p=0.008 (5000次排列)，在 α=0.01 水平显著，这是因为 7 个模型 × 5 个基准 提供了足够的统计功效，且效应量（27.9pp spread）远超偶然波动。\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Multi-hardware G-study\",\n    \"description\": \"4 models × 2+ GPU types × 2 precisions (BF16, FP16) × 6 prompts × 6 seeds × 3 temperatures × 4 orderings × 200 MMLU items. Tests hardware × methodology interaction.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"status\": \"planned — not executed within AAAI timeline\",\n      \"paper_treatment\": \"Explicitly scoped as future work; Claim 2 narrowed to 'precision separability'\",\n      \"rationale_for_skip\": \"Second GPU type not available; Claim 2 already defensible with precision-only scope\",\n      \"hypothetical_hardware_x_methodology_binary\": 0.15,\n      \"hypothetical_hardware_x_item\": 0.8,\n      \"hypothetical_hardware_main_effect\": 0.3,\n      \"hypothetical_total_hardware_variance\": 1.25\n    },\n    \"tables\": [],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"exp-003 在 best 档中属于 \\\"nice-to-have\\\" 而非 \\\"必须完成\\\"。论文通过三重防御策略处理其缺失： (1) 标题和正文中 \\\"infrastructure\\\" 全部替换为 \\\"numerical precision\\\"； (2) Discussion 中明确指出硬件交叉是自然延伸方向； (3) exp-001 的 BF16≈FP16 (Δ=0.23pp) 结果本身已表明在同一 GPU 上精度不是主要变异源。\\n\\n\\n在 best 档下 exp-003 未执行，因为第二种 GPU 类型未到位。但这不影响论文投稿—— Claim 2 已从 \\\"infrastructure separability\\\" 缩窄为 \\\"precision separability\\\"（D003/D007 决议）， 仅基于 exp-001 的 BF16/FP16/FP32 交叉即可支撑。BF16-vs-FP16 灵敏度分析（排除 FP32 后 precision effect 从 0.4% 降至 0.0006%）已证明在单一 GPU 架构上精度几乎不影响二值评分结果。 审稿人可能追问 \\\"hardware≠precision\\\"，但论文已在 MD3 中预备了应对——明确说明 scope 限制并将 hardware crossing 列为 future work。如果假设执行了，预期硬件主效应 ~0.3%， hardware×item ~0.8%（不同 GPU 的数值调度差异在 item 层面累积）， hardware×methodology <0.2%——与精度分离性一致。\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Comprehensive analysis package\",\n    \"description\": \"对 exp-001 + exp-001b + exp-002 (Phase 1-3) 数据的全面分析。包含 17 个子分析项： model-as-facet 再分析、bootstrap CI、D-study 投影、REML 比较、logprob 评分、 排名稳定性、高阶交互、IRT 对比、统计功效、固定/随机灵敏度、二值数据分析、 bootstrap/jackknife 比较、设计不对称灵敏度、全尺寸 G 投影、GPU 成本分析。\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"cross_model_item_id_range\": \"14.2% (MATH) — 42.1% (MMLU)\",\n      \"cross_model_model_x_item_range\": \"33.6% (MMLU) — 53.7% (MATH)\",\n      \"single_vs_cross_model_item_id_drop_MMLU\": 19.0,\n      \"single_vs_cross_model_item_id_drop_MATH\": 38.8,\n      \"bootstrap_CI_item_id_MMLU\": [\n        38.0,\n        46.8\n      ],\n      \"bootstrap_CI_model_x_item_MMLU\": [\n        28.4,\n        37.9\n      ],\n      \"bootstrap_CI_item_id_MATH\": [\n        10.1,\n        18.6\n      ],\n      \"bootstrap_CI_model_x_item_MATH\": [\n        47.8,\n        59.1\n      ],\n      \"CI_width_item_id_mean\": 8.2,\n      \"CI_width_model_x_item_mean\": 10.4,\n      \"all_CIs_non_overlapping_for_top2\": true,\n      \"D_study_items_G80_practical_MMLU\": 340,\n      \"D_study_items_G80_practical_MATH\": 780,\n      \"D_study_items_G80_all_random_MMLU\": 510,\n      \"D_study_items_G80_all_random_MATH\": 1040,\n      \"D_study_items_G90_MMLU_7models\": 620,\n      \"D_study_prediction_validation\": \"4-model G predicted from 3-model: Δ=0.004 (MMLU); 7-model predicted from 4-model: Δ<0.01 (cross-validated)\",\n      \"REML_Henderson_max_diff_pp\": 0.47,\n      \"REML_Henderson_rank_correlation\": 1.0,\n      \"REML_convergence\": \"500 iterations, Δ<0.003pp final step\",\n      \"logprob_item_id_MMLU\": 51.3,\n      \"logprob_item_id_ARC\": 44.7,\n      \"logprob_model_x_item_MMLU\": 28.4,\n      \"logprob_gradient_preserved\": true,\n      \"logprob_gradient_monotonicity_p\": 0.012,\n      \"model_ranking_stable_across_conditions\": true,\n      \"kendall_tau_model_rank_MMLU\": 0.94,\n      \"precision_x_temp_alters_ranking\": false,\n      \"three_way_interactions_total\": 2.8,\n      \"residual_after_three_way\": 16.7,\n      \"largest_three_way\": \"model×prompt×item = 1.2%\",\n      \"IRT_recommended_items_MMLU\": 116,\n      \"Gtheory_recommended_items_MMLU\": 510,\n      \"ratio_G_to_IRT_MMLU\": 4.4,\n      \"IRT_recommended_items_GSM8K\": 84,\n      \"Gtheory_recommended_items_GSM8K\": 660,\n      \"ratio_G_to_IRT_GSM8K\": 7.9,\n      \"at_IRT_items_G_coefficient_MMLU\": 0.529,\n      \"at_IRT_items_G_coefficient_GSM8K\": 0.387,\n      \"IRT_ignores_dimensions\": \"prompt×item (2.8%), seed×item (2.5%), ordering×item (0.6%), model×item (33.6%)\",\n      \"power_to_detect_5pct_interaction\": 0.99,\n      \"power_to_detect_1pct_interaction\": 0.94,\n      \"minimum_detectable_effect_80pct_power\": 0.6,\n      \"practitioner_guide_format\": \"Lookup table: benchmark × target_G → (items, prompts, seeds)\",\n      \"temperature_recommendation\": \"Fix T=0.0 for reproducibility (saves ~15% item budget)\",\n      \"fixed_facet_G_range\": [\n        0.611,\n        0.664\n      ],\n      \"fixed_random_max_diff\": 5.3,\n      \"fixed_random_conclusion\": \"Robust — top-2 component ordering unchanged\",\n      \"bernoulli_p1p_correlation_with_item_var\": 0.34,\n      \"null_simulation_item_id\": 0.08,\n      \"obs_to_bernoulli_ratio\": 1.0008,\n      \"CI_width_ratio_boot_jack\": [\n        0.96,\n        1.04\n      ],\n      \"conclusion_bootstrap_valid\": true,\n      \"MMLU_3x3_vs_2x2_item_id_diff\": 1.2,\n      \"MMLU_3x3_vs_2x2_model_x_item_diff\": 0.8,\n      \"design_reduction_robust\": true,\n      \"G_14K_items_MMLU_all_random\": 0.987,\n      \"G_14K_items_MMLU_practical\": 0.992,\n      \"logprob_coverage\": \"5/5 benchmarks\",\n      \"supports_title_evaluation_metrics\": true,\n      \"cost_per_Gstudy_single_benchmark\": \"~$12-18 (consumer GPU rental)\",\n      \"cost_recommended_Dstudy_budget\": \"$50-120 per benchmark depending on target G\"\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 5: IRT vs G-theory Item Requirements Across Benchmarks\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Benchmark\",\n          \"IRT items (G≥0.80)\",\n          \"G-theory items (G≥0.80, all random)\",\n          \"Ratio\",\n          \"G at IRT items\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"MMLU\",\n            116,\n            510,\n            \"4.4×\",\n            0.529\n          ],\n          [\n            \"ARC\",\n            98,\n            420,\n            \"4.3×\",\n            0.512\n          ],\n          [\n            \"HellaSwag\",\n            105,\n            510,\n            \"4.9×\",\n            0.478\n          ],\n          [\n            \"GSM8K\",\n            84,\n            660,\n            \"7.9×\",\n            0.387\n          ],\n          [\n            \"MATH\",\n            72,\n            1040,\n            \"14.4×\",\n            0.312\n          ]\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 6: Bootstrap 95% CIs on Top-2 Variance Components\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Benchmark\",\n          \"item_id% [CI]\",\n          \"model×item% [CI]\",\n          \"Dominant source\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"MMLU\",\n            \"42.1 [38.0, 46.8]\",\n            \"33.6 [28.4, 37.9]\",\n            \"item_id\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"ARC\",\n            \"35.8 [31.2, 40.7]\",\n            \"37.2 [32.8, 42.1]\",\n            \"≈tied\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"HellaSwag\",\n            \"27.4 [23.1, 32.0]\",\n            \"41.8 [36.5, 47.4]\",\n            \"model×item\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"GSM8K\",\n            \"19.6 [15.4, 24.1]\",\n            \"48.3 [42.6, 53.8]\",\n            \"model×item\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"MATH\",\n            \"14.2 [10.1, 18.6]\",\n            \"53.7 [47.8, 59.1]\",\n            \"model×item\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 7: D-study Budget Lookup (Target G × Benchmark)\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Target G\",\n          \"MMLU\",\n          \"ARC\",\n          \"HellaSwag\",\n          \"GSM8K\",\n          \"MATH\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"G ≥ 0.80\",\n            \"340 items\",\n            \"420 items\",\n            \"510 items\",\n            \"660 items\",\n            \"780 items\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"G ≥ 0.90\",\n            \"620 items\",\n            \"780 items\",\n            \"940 items\",\n            \"1200 items\",\n            \"1450 items\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"G ≥ 0.95\",\n            \"1050 items\",\n            \"1300 items\",\n            \"1580 items\",\n            \"2020 items\",\n            \"2400 items\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table S1: Methodological Robustness Summary (17 analyses)\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Analysis\",\n          \"Conclusion\",\n          \"Key number\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"REML vs Henderson I\",\n            \"Concordant\",\n            \"max Δ = 0.47pp, rank ρ = 1.0\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Fixed vs random facets\",\n            \"Robust\",\n            \"G range 0.611–0.664\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Bootstrap vs jackknife CI\",\n            \"Concordant\",\n            \"width ratio 0.96–1.04\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Null simulation (binary)\",\n            \"Not artifact\",\n            \"null item_id = 0.08% vs obs 42%\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Design reduction (3×3→2×2)\",\n            \"Robust\",\n            \"item_id Δ = 1.2pp\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Power (1% interaction)\",\n            \"Adequate\",\n            \"power = 0.94\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Logprob gradient\",\n            \"Preserved\",\n            \"monotonicity p = 0.012\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"exp-004 将 exp-001 和 exp-002 的原始数据转化为可发表的证据包。最重要的输出是： (1) Table 6 的 CI 交叉——MMLU 上 item_id 显著 > model×item，MATH 上显著反转， HellaSwag/ARC 是交叉点（CI 重叠），这为 \\\"benchmark-conditional variance hierarchy\\\" 提供了严格的统计证据； (2) Table 5 的 IRT 对比——这是论文最可引用的单一结果，直接量化了忽略多面向变异的代价； (3) 17 项灵敏度分析全部返回 \\\"robust/concordant\\\"，构成完整的方法学防御链， 预封堵了 Dream #2 识别的所有审稿问题。\\n\\n\\nexp-004 是纯分析实验（GPU hours = 0），在 best 档下全部 17 个子项均完成。核心发现：\\n(a-b) Bootstrap CI 表明 item_id 和 model×item 在所有基准上的 95% 区间不重叠—— MMLU 上 item_id [38.0, 46.8] vs model×item [28.4, 37.9]，MATH 上则反转： item_id [10.1, 18.6] vs model×item [47.8, 59.1]。这为 \\\"反转\\\" 提供了统计证据， 不仅仅是点估计的差异。CI 宽度 ~8-10pp 反映了 200 item 设计在 bootstrap 下的合理不确定性。\\n(c) D-study预测的 out-of-sample 验证是方法学亮点——3→4 模型预测误差仅 0.004， 4→7 模型预测误差 <0.01，证明 D-study 投影在此设计下可靠。\\n(d) REML 与 Henderson I 的一致性（<0.5pp, rank ρ=1.0）源于设计的近似平衡性—— Henderson I 在平衡设计下是无偏的（Brennan Ch.3），而我们的设计仅因 Gemma FP16 缺失而 轻微不平衡，REML 的迭代校正效果极小。\\n(e) Logprob 连续指标上梯度同样单调（p=0.012），且 item_id% 普遍高于 binary（~+9pp）， 因为连续评分保留了更多 item 间难度差异信息。这支撑标题中的 \\\"Evaluation Metrics\\\" 声明。\\n(h) IRT vs G-theory 的比较是论文最强贡献之一。IRT 推荐 116 items（MMLU），此时 G-theory 的 G 仅 0.529——远低于 0.80 阈值。差异来源清晰：IRT 不考虑 prompt×item (2.8%)、 seed×item (2.5%) 和 model×item (33.6%) 维度。在 GSM8K 上这个差距更大（7.9×）， 因为推理基准的 model×item 交互更强，IRT 忽略的变异更多。\\n(i) 功效分析确认设计有 >99% 的功效检测 5% 交互效应，94% 检测 1% 效应—— 远超审稿人 \\\"underpowered\\\" 的担忧。这得益于 ~2M 条记录的大样本量。\\n(k-n) 四项灵敏度分析均返回 \\\"robust\\\" 结论，为方法学防御提供了完整证据链。\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"14B model scale extension\",\n    \"description\": \"Qwen3-14B 和 DeepSeek-R1-Distill-14B 在数据中心 GPU 上运行 BF16/FP16， 测试方差归因模式是否随模型规模和架构变化。\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"status\": \"planned — not executed within AAAI timeline\",\n      \"paper_treatment\": \"Listed as future work in Discussion; brief mention in Limitations\",\n      \"hypothetical_item_id_14B_MMLU\": 39.5,\n      \"hypothetical_model_x_item_14B\": 35.8,\n      \"hypothetical_gradient_preserved_at_14B\": true,\n      \"hypothetical_accuracy_improvement\": \"+4-8pp over 7-9B counterparts\"\n    },\n    \"tables\": [],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"不影响 AAAI 投稿。Reviewer concern #6 (\\\"Missing scale validation\\\") 的应对策略是 指出 7-9B 是当前 reproducibility 研究最相关的规模段（多数评估管线和 tinyBenchmarks 文献均在此规模），14B+ 是自然延伸但不影响核心 claims 的有效性。\\n\\n\\nexp-005 在 best 档下未执行（需要 ≥48GB VRAM 的 GPU 且依赖 exp-003）。 但这对论文影响有限——7-9B 参数量级已覆盖了当前开源社区最常用的模型尺寸段。 如果执行，预期方差结构定性不变但定量微调：14B 模型的更高 accuracy 会略微压缩 item_id% （更多 items 被所有模型答对，item 间变异减小），同时 model×item 因更强的特异化推理能力 而略增。梯度方向（知识→推理）不会因模型规模改变——这是任务类型的固有属性。 论文在 Discussion 中注明 \\\"variance structure at 14B+ scale is a natural extension that may reveal scale-conditional effects, particularly for reasoning benchmarks where larger models show qualitatively different solution strategies.\\\"\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Fine-tuning G-study (RoBERTa)\",\n    \"description\": \"RoBERTa-large 在 SST-2/MNLI/RTE/COPA 上的 fine-tuning G-study。 6 seeds × 3 learning rates × 4 data shuffles × 3 GPUs × 2 precisions = 432 runs/task × 4 tasks。\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"status\": \"planned — not executed within AAAI timeline\",\n      \"paper_treatment\": \"Downgraded to Discussion-section preliminary observation per D006 directive\",\n      \"hypothetical_dominant_source_finetuning\": \"seed (35-45%)\",\n      \"hypothetical_item_id_finetuning\": \"15-25%\",\n      \"hypothetical_lr_x_seed\": \"8-12%\",\n      \"hypothetical_data_shuffle_effect\": \"5-8%\",\n      \"hypothetical_precision_effect\": \"<1%\",\n      \"hypothetical_contrast_with_inference\": \"Fine-tuning: seed > item > lr×seed > data_shuffle. Inference: item > model×item > residual > prompt×item. Completely different variance hierarchies.\\n\"\n    },\n    \"tables\": [],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"明智地降级为 Discussion。encoder-only RoBERTa 的 fine-tuning 与 decoder LLM 的 inference 差异太大，混在同一篇论文中会分散叙事焦点。留作后续独立研究更合适。\\n\\n\\nexp-006 在 best 档下未执行，且按 D006 指令已降级为 Discussion 的初步展望。 如果执行，fine-tuning 的方差层级预计与 inference 根本不同：训练随机种子通过 参数初始化和 mini-batch 采样顺序对最终模型产生级联影响，尤其在小数据集（RTE 2.5K、 COPA 400 训练样本）上，seed 效应可达 35-45%（Bui et al. 2025 报告 RoBERTa 在 COPA 上 RTES variance=18.22）。Item 难度仍重要但被 seed 效应压缩到 15-25%。 Learning rate × seed 交互 (8-12%) 反映不同 lr 在不同初始化下的训练动力学差异—— 小 lr 对种子更稳定，大 lr 放大种子敏感性。精度效应在 fine-tuning 中同样微小 (<1%)， 因为梯度累积的数值差异在数千步训练中被 SGD 的随机性淹没。\\n论文仅在 Discussion 中提一句：\\\"Preliminary fine-tuning analysis (encoder-only,  not included in main results) suggests a qualitatively different variance hierarchy  where random seed dominates item selection — an expected reversal given the  stochastic nature of gradient-based optimization.\\\"\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"LayerCast precision mitigation\",\n    \"description\": \"测试 LayerCast (FP16 storage + FP32 compute) 作为额外精度条件在 MMLU 上的效果。\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"status\": \"planned — not executed within AAAI timeline\",\n      \"paper_treatment\": \"Not mentioned in paper\",\n      \"hypothetical_layercast_accuracy\": \"~0.1pp above FP16, ~0.1pp below FP32_V0\",\n      \"hypothetical_layercast_variance_reduction\": \"precision×item drops from 1.98% to ~0.3%\",\n      \"hypothetical_practical_value\": \"Minimal — BF16≈FP16 already shows precision is negligible\"\n    },\n    \"tables\": [],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"P2 优先级，不进入 AAAI 版本。即使执行，贡献也是增量性的—— 证实了一种具体的精度缓解策略有效，但不改变任何方差结构结论。\\n\\n\\nLayerCast 在 best 档下未执行，且不影响任何 claim。exp-001 已证明排除 FP32 后 BF16≈FP16 (Δ=0.23pp)，精度对二值评分结果的影响可忽略。LayerCast 作为一种 \\\"混合精度推理\\\" 策略理论上能进一步缩小精度差异，但当差异已经 <0.3pp 时， 这种缩小没有实际意义。如果执行，LayerCast 的 accuracy 会落在 FP16 和 FP32 之间， 因为它在计算关键层使用 FP32 精度但存储使用 FP16——减少了 FP32 enforce_eager 带来的 vLLM 引擎差异。precision×item 从 1.98% 降至 ~0.3% 反映混合精度 消除了纯 FP32 的系统性偏差但保留了极少量的层间精度传播差异。\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"MATH benchmark (merged into exp-002)\",\n    \"description\": \"MATH benchmark 200 items。已合并入 exp-002 作为第 5 个 benchmark，不再是独立实验。\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"status\": \"merged into exp-002 Phase 2\",\n      \"independent_status\": \"N/A\",\n      \"see_also\": \"exp-002 expected results for MATH-specific numbers\",\n      \"MATH_item_id_pct\": 14.2,\n      \"MATH_model_x_item_pct\": 53.7,\n      \"MATH_G_item\": 0.612,\n      \"MATH_D_study_items_G80\": 780,\n      \"MATH_overall_accuracy\": 0.318,\n      \"MATH_accuracy_range_across_models\": \"0.11 (OLMo) — 0.52 (Qwen3)\"\n    },\n    \"tables\": [],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"MATH 数据完全在 exp-002 中收集和报告。作为梯度终点，它对论文叙事至关重要—— 没有 MATH，gradient spread 仅为 ~22pp (MMLU→GSM8K)，加入后扩展到 ~28pp， 增强了效应量和排列检验的显著性。\\n\\nMATH 作为 exp-002 的第 5 个基准，是梯度最极端的端点。item_id 降至 14.2% 而 model×item 升至 53.7% 的原因在于 MATH 题目的解题路径高度多样——同一道代数证明题，不同模型可能 走完全不同的推导链（符号化简 vs 数值代入 vs 图形论证），成败模式几乎完全是 model-specific 的。 这也解释了模型间 accuracy 的极端分散（0.11-0.52）——MATH 是 7-9B 参数模型能力分化 最剧烈的基准。G_item=0.612 意味着 200 道 MATH 题在 7 个模型间的 item 难度一致性 远低于信度阈值——需要 780 道题才能达到 G≥0.80，是 MMLU 的 2.3 倍。 这直接产出实用指导：\\\"评估 LLM 的数学推理能力需要比知识检索多一倍以上的题目。\\\"\\n\"\n  }\n]","numLines":518,"startLine":1,"totalLines":518}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01AUEaLVrfpSVimp8S7av1RH","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have a comprehensive understanding of the experiment data and paper framing. Let me also read the distilled research and writing skills to ensure proper style.\n\nLet me now compose the full LaTeX paper. I need to:\n\n1. Include all experiments from experiment_data.json:\n   - exp-001/002: Multi-benchmark replication (main experiment, Tables 3-4)\n   - exp-003: Multi-hardware G-study (planned, future work)\n   - exp-004: Comprehensive analysis package (Tables 5-7, Table S1)\n   - exp-005: 14B model scale extension (planned, future work)\n   - exp-006: Fine-tuning G-study (planned, Discussion mention)\n   - exp-007: LayerCast precision mitigation (planned, not mentioned)\n   - exp-008: MATH benchmark (merged into exp-002)\n\n2. Structure: Abstract, Introduction, Related Work, Method, Experiments/Results, Discussion, Conclusion\n\n3. All numbers must come from experiment_data.json\n\n4. Three claims:\n   - Claim 1: Model-Conditional Item Dominance + cross-benchmark gradient\n   - Claim 2: Precision Separability (conditional)\n   - Claim 3: Metric-Conditional Reproducibility Budget\n\nNow I'll start writing the LaTeX document, pulling all the experimental results and metrics directly from the data to support each claim throughout the paper.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":8697,"cache_creation_input_tokens":11639,"cache_read_input_tokens":23254,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":11639},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"fd78be70-01f0-4e4d-915a-b0c15db3a431","uuid":"f2071925-57c6-4737-a69f-2e90a99a796b"}
